The Dunmer, or Dark Elves, are the grey-skinned, red-eyed natives of Morrowind, known for their intellect, agility, and mastery of both combat and magic. Their culture values loyalty to family and clan, with a reputation for being grim, aloof, and distrustful of outsiders. While often perceived as harsh and vengeful due to a history of conflict and betrayal, they hold honor in high regard. Dunmer raised in Morrowind are more traditional, while those integrated into Imperial culture are seen as more approachable. Their long lifespans and resilience reflect their ability to thrive in their harsh homeland.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('altmer', 'The Altmer, commonly known as High Elves, are a tall, golden-skinned race originating from the Summerset Isles. Renowned for their mastery of the arcane arts, they are considered the most gifted in magic among all races of Tamriel. The Altmer possess long lifespans, living two to three times longer than humans, which allows them to dedicate many years to the study of magic and scholarly pursuits. Known for their intelligence and cultured way of life, the Altmer have contributed greatly to Tamriel''s language, laws, arts, and sciences, with much of the Empire’s customs derived from their traditions.

Despite their contributions, the Altmer are often viewed with suspicion and resentment by other races, who see them as proud, elitist, and aloof. This perception is reinforced by the Altmer''s own belief in their cultural superiority, which has caused friction between them and the other inhabitants of Tamriel. Nevertheless, the Altmer''s influence, particularly in magic and intellectual endeavors, remains a defining aspect of their legacy in the continent.', '''all'':38B ''allows'':56B ''aloof'':125B ''altmer'':1A,3B,43B,80B,107B,132B,154B ''among'':37B ''and'':67B,74B,90B,113B,124B,146B,160B ''arcan'':27B ''are'':9B,30B,108B ''arts'':28B,89B ''as'':6B,121B ''aspect'':166B ''belief'':135B ''betw'':144B ''by'':115B,130B ''caus'':142B ''commonly'':4B ''consider'':31B ''continent'':172B ''contribut'':82B ''contributions'':105B ''cultur'':75B ''cultural'':138B ''customs'':98B ''dedicat'':59B ''defining'':165B ''deriv'':99B ''despit'':103B ''elitist'':123B ''elves'':8B ''empir'':96B ''endeavors'':162B ''for'':22B,71B ''friction'':143B ''from'':17B,100B ''gift'':34B ''gold'':13B ''golden-skinn'':12B ''greatly'':83B ''hav'':81B ''high'':7B ''humans'':54B ''in'':35B,136B,158B,170B ''influenc'':156B ''inhabitants'':149B ''intellectual'':161B ''intelligenc'':73B ''is'':128B ''isles'':20B ''known'':5B,70B ''languag'':87B ''laws'':88B ''legacy'':169B ''lif'':78B ''lifespans'':46B ''living'':47B ''long'':45B,52B ''magic'':36B,66B,159B ''many'':60B ''mastery'':24B ''most'':33B ''much'':93B ''nevertheless'':152B ''of'':25B,40B,65B,77B,94B,150B,167B ''often'':109B ''originating'':16B ''other'':116B,148B ''own'':134B ''particularly'':157B ''perception'':127B ''possess'':44B ''proud'':122B ''pursuits'':69B ''rac'':15B,39B,117B ''reinforc'':129B ''remains'':163B ''renown'':21B ''resentment'':114B ''s'':86B,97B,133B,155B ''scholarly'':68B ''scienc'':91B ''see'':119B ''skinn'':14B ''study'':64B ''summerset'':19B ''superiority'':139B ''suspicion'':112B ''tall'':11B ''tamriel'':41B,85B,151B ''than'':53B ''the'':2B,18B,23B,26B,32B,42B,63B,72B,79B,95B,101B,104B,106B,131B,137B,147B,153B,168B,171B ''them'':57B,120B,145B ''they'':29B ''this'':126B ''thre'':50B ''tim'':51B ''to'':49B,58B,62B,84B ''traditions'':102B ''two'':48B ''view'':110B ''way'':76B ''which'':55B,140B ''who'':118B ''with'':92B,111B ''years'':61B', 'highelf, scholar', 'The Altmer, or High Elves, are a tall, golden-skinned race from the Summerset Isles, renowned for their unparalleled mastery of magic and intellectual pursuits. Their long lifespans enable them to excel in the arcane arts, scholarship, and culture, with much of Tamriel''s language, laws, and traditions influenced by their contributions. While admired for their achievements, they are often seen as aloof and elitist due to their belief in their cultural superiority, causing tension with other races. Despite this, their impact on magic and civilization is undeniable.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('khajiit', 'The Khajiit are a feline race native to the province of Elsweyr, known for their agility, intelligence, and distinctive cat-like appearance. They are skilled in acrobatics, stealth, and make excellent thieves, but they are also capable warriors and traders. Although Khajiit are rarely mages, their unique physical traits, such as fur, tails, and digitigrade walking, set them apart from both men and mer. These differences have led to their classification as one of Tamriel''s "beast races," alongside Argonians and Imga. The Khajiit are known for their love of moon sugar, an integral part of their culture and economy, which is often refined into the addictive substance skooma.

Khajiit society is structured around clans and their relationship with the phases of Nirn''s moons, Masser and Secunda, which influence their physiology and determine their form upon birth. The Khajiit exhibit a wide variety of shapes, from bipedal, human-like figures to quadrupedal, more animalistic forms. Their homeland of Elsweyr is a land of deserts and jungles, where they have developed a rich cultural heritage with distinct architecture, art, and traditions. Despite facing racial prejudice across Tamriel, Khajiit are proud of their heritage and the unique contributions they bring to Tamrielic society.', '''acrobatics'':29B ''across'':189B ''addictiv'':109B ''agility'':17B ''alongsid'':81B ''also'':38B ''although'':43B ''an'':95B ''and'':19B,31B,41B,56B,65B,83B,101B,118B,129B,135B,169B,183B,197B ''animalistic'':158B ''apart'':61B ''appearanc'':24B ''architectur'':181B ''are'':4B,26B,37B,45B,87B,192B ''argonians'':82B ''around'':116B ''art'':182B ''as'':53B,74B ''beast'':79B ''bipedal'':150B ''birth'':140B ''both'':63B ''bring'':202B ''but'':35B ''capabl'':39B ''cat'':22B ''cat-lik'':21B ''clans'':117B ''classification'':73B ''contributions'':200B ''cultur'':100B ''cultural'':177B ''deserts'':168B ''despit'':185B ''determin'':136B ''develop'':174B ''differenc'':68B ''digitigrad'':57B ''distinct'':180B ''distinctiv'':20B ''economy'':102B ''elsweyr'':13B,163B ''excellent'':33B ''exhibit'':143B ''facing'':186B ''felin'':6B ''figur'':154B ''for'':15B,89B ''form'':138B ''forms'':159B ''from'':62B,149B ''fur'':54B ''hav'':69B,173B ''heritag'':178B,196B ''homeland'':161B ''hum'':152B ''human-lik'':151B ''imga'':84B ''in'':28B ''influenc'':132B ''integral'':96B ''intelligenc'':18B ''into'':107B ''is'':104B,114B,164B ''jungl'':170B ''khajiit'':1A,3B,44B,86B,112B,142B,191B ''known'':14B,88B ''land'':166B ''led'':70B ''lik'':23B,153B ''lov'':91B ''mag'':47B ''mak'':32B ''mass'':128B ''men'':64B ''mer'':66B ''moon'':93B ''moons'':127B ''mor'':157B ''nativ'':8B ''nirn'':125B ''of'':12B,76B,92B,98B,124B,147B,162B,167B,194B ''often'':105B ''one'':75B ''part'':97B ''phas'':123B ''physical'':50B ''physiology'':134B ''prejudic'':188B ''proud'':193B ''provinc'':11B ''quadrupedal'':156B ''rac'':7B,80B ''racial'':187B ''rarely'':46B ''refin'':106B ''relationship'':120B ''rich'':176B ''s'':78B,126B ''secund'':130B ''set'':59B ''shap'':148B ''skill'':27B ''skoom'':111B ''society'':113B,205B ''stealth'':30B ''structur'':115B ''substanc'':110B ''such'':52B ''sug'':94B ''tails'':55B ''tamriel'':77B,190B ''tamrielic'':204B ''the'':2B,10B,16B,48B,72B,85B,90B,99B,108B,119B,122B,133B,137B,141B,160B,195B,198B ''them'':60B ''thes'':67B ''they'':25B,36B,172B,201B ''thiev'':34B ''to'':9B,71B,155B,203B ''traders'':42B ''traditions'':184B ''traits'':51B ''uniqu'':49B,199B ''upon'':139B ''variety'':146B ''walking'':58B ''warriors'':40B ''wher'':171B ''which'':103B,131B ''wid'':145B ''with'':121B,179B', 'khajiit, scholar', '
The Khajiit are a feline race from Elsweyr, known for their agility, stealth, and distinctive cat-like traits such as fur, tails, and digitigrade walking. Skilled as thieves, warriors, and traders, they are tied to the moons Masser and Secunda, which influence their form at birth. Their culture revolves around moon sugar, central to their economy and traditions, though its refinement into skooma has caused controversy. Despite facing prejudice, the Khajiit take pride in their rich heritage, vibrant arts, and unique contributions to Tamrielic society.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('bound_weapon', 'These magical effects are offensive Conjuration spells that summon spectral weapons, such as swords or bows, crafted from Daedric energy. This effect is only available as a spell, offering battlemages weightless, unbreakable weapons that cannot be disarmed.', '''are'':6B ''as'':15B,28B ''avail'':27B ''battlemag'':32B ''be'':38B ''bound'':1A ''bows'':18B ''cannot'':37B ''conjuration'':8B ''craft'':19B ''daedric'':21B ''disarm'':39B ''effect'':24B ''effects'':5B ''energy'':22B ''from'':20B ''is'':25B ''magical'':4B ''offensiv'':7B ''offering'':31B ''only'':26B ''or'':17B ''spectral'':12B ''spell'':30B ''spells'':9B ''such'':14B ''summon'':11B ''swords'':16B ''that'':10B,36B ''thes'':3B ''this'':23B ''unbreak'':34B ''weapon'':2A ''weapons'':13B,35B ''weightless'':33B', 'mage', 'This magic creates a weapon out of thin air, like a glowing sword or bow, to use in a fight. A mage might know more though.', '', '', 'spells');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('nord', 'Nords, also known as the Children of the Sky, are a race of tall, fair-haired humans from the province of Skyrim, renowned for their resistance to cold and magical frost. Known as fierce and powerful warriors, Nords excel in all forms of combat, often serving as soldiers, mercenaries, or adventurers across Tamriel. Their culture is deeply martial, and their history is filled with tales of conquests and warfare, dating back to their migration from the northern land of Atmora. Though once famed for their seafaring prowess, the Nords have since established their homeland of Skyrim, where they are fiercely protective of their traditions and independence.

Nordic society is built around a strong sense of honor, loyalty, and ancestor worship. Their religion, known as the Old Ways, venerates a pantheon of gods, including Kyne, the goddess of the sky, and Shor, the god of the underworld. Nords also revere the Thu’um, the power of the Voice, a mystical force used by legendary warriors known as Tongues. Nords have a distinct architectural style, marked by wooden longhouses and fortresses, and their funerary customs emphasize ancestor worship, with burial sites treated as sacred places. Their cultural heritage and warrior spirit define much of their identity, making them one of Tamriel’s most formidable and respected peoples.', '''across'':54B ''adventurers'':53B ''all'':43B ''also'':3B,150B ''ancestor'':121B,187B ''and'':31B,37B,61B,70B,107B,120B,142B,180B,182B,199B,215B ''architectural'':174B ''are'':11B,101B ''around'':113B ''as'':5B,35B,49B,126B,168B,193B ''atmor'':82B ''back'':73B ''built'':112B ''burial'':190B ''by'':164B,177B ''childr'':7B ''cold'':30B ''combat'':46B ''conquests'':69B ''cultur'':57B ''cultural'':197B ''customs'':185B ''dating'':72B ''deeply'':59B ''defin'':202B ''distinct'':173B ''emphasiz'':186B ''establish'':94B ''excel'':41B ''fair'':17B ''fair-hair'':16B ''fam'':85B ''fierc'':36B ''fiercely'':102B ''fill'':65B ''for'':26B,86B ''forc'':162B ''formid'':214B ''forms'':44B ''fortress'':181B ''from'':20B,77B ''frost'':33B ''funerary'':184B ''god'':145B ''goddess'':138B ''gods'':134B ''hair'':18B ''hav'':92B,171B ''heritag'':198B ''history'':63B ''homeland'':96B ''honor'':118B ''humans'':19B ''identity'':206B ''in'':42B ''including'':135B ''independenc'':108B ''is'':58B,64B,111B ''known'':4B,34B,125B,167B ''kyne'':136B ''land'':80B ''legendary'':165B ''longhous'':179B ''loyalty'':119B ''magical'':32B ''making'':207B ''mark'':176B ''martial'':60B ''mercenari'':51B ''migration'':76B ''most'':213B ''much'':203B ''mystical'':161B ''nord'':1A ''nordic'':109B ''nords'':2B,40B,91B,149B,170B ''northern'':79B ''of'':8B,14B,23B,45B,68B,81B,97B,104B,117B,133B,139B,146B,157B,204B,210B ''often'':47B ''old'':128B ''once'':84B ''one'':209B ''or'':52B ''pantheon'':132B ''peopl'':217B ''plac'':195B ''pow'':156B ''powerful'':38B ''protectiv'':103B ''provinc'':22B ''prowess'':89B ''rac'':13B ''religion'':124B ''renown'':25B ''resistanc'':28B ''respect'':216B ''rever'':151B ''s'':212B ''sacr'':194B ''seafaring'':88B ''sens'':116B ''serving'':48B ''shor'':143B ''sinc'':93B ''sit'':191B ''sky'':10B,141B ''skyrim'':24B,98B ''society'':110B ''soldiers'':50B ''spirit'':201B ''strong'':115B ''style'':175B ''tal'':67B ''tall'':15B ''tamriel'':55B,211B ''the'':6B,9B,21B,27B,56B,62B,75B,78B,87B,90B,95B,105B,123B,127B,137B,140B,144B,147B,152B,155B,158B,183B,196B,205B ''them'':208B ''they'':100B ''though'':83B ''thu'':153B ''to'':29B,74B ''tong'':169B ''traditions'':106B ''treat'':192B ''um'':154B ''underworld'':148B ''used'':163B ''venerat'':130B ''voic'':159B ''warfar'':71B ''warrior'':200B ''warriors'':39B,166B ''ways'':129B ''wher'':99B ''with'':66B,189B ''wood'':178B ''worship'':122B,188B', 'nord, dragon, scholar', '
Nords, or the Children of the Sky, are tall, fair-haired humans from Skyrim, known for their resistance to cold and frost magic. Renowned as fierce warriors, they excel in combat and value honor, loyalty, and independence. Their culture, rooted in ancestor worship and the Old Ways, venerates gods like Kyne and Shor and honors the mystical Thu’um, or Voice. With a rich history of warfare and a distinct architectural style of wooden longhouses, Nords remain a proud and formidable people, deeply connected to their traditions and homeland.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('oblivion_crisis', 'The Oblivion Crisis, also known as the Great Anguish, was a catastrophic conflict between the Daedric hordes of Mehrunes Dagon and the people of Tamriel, beginning in the year 3E 433. The crisis was ignited by the assassination of Emperor Uriel Septim VII and his heirs, leading to the opening of Oblivion Gates across the continent, through which Daedra emerged in overwhelming numbers. These gates were facilitated by the Mythic Dawn, a fanatical cult devoted to Mehrunes Dagon, resulting in widespread devastation throughout various provinces. Key regions such as Skyrim faced brutal sieges, while Kvatch in Cyrodiil was completely destroyed. The Argonians of Black Marsh, called upon by the Hist, mounted a successful counter-offensive against the Daedra, forcing them to close their gates in that region.

Despite the turmoil, the Empire struggled to maintain order, with significant forces withdrawn from provinces like Morrowind, leaving Cyrodiil largely defenseless. Amidst this chaos, Martin Septim, the last heir of the Septim bloodline, emerged as a pivotal figure. He joined forces with a hero of destiny to confront the Daedric threat. In a climactic sacrifice, Martin transformed into an Avatar of Akatosh, utilizing the power of the Amulet of Kings to banish Mehrunes Dagon back to Oblivion, thus ending the crisis and marking the conclusion of the Third Era.', '''3e'':32B ''433'':33B ''across'':56B ''against'':119B ''akatosh'':192B ''also'':6B ''amidst'':152B ''amulet'':198B ''an'':189B ''and'':23B,46B,212B ''anguish'':11B ''argonians'':104B ''as'':8B,91B,165B ''assassination'':40B ''avat'':190B ''back'':205B ''banish'':202B ''beginning'':28B ''betw'':16B ''black'':106B ''bloodlin'':163B ''brutal'':94B ''by'':38B,70B,110B ''call'':108B ''catastrophic'':14B ''cha'':154B ''climactic'':184B ''clos'':125B ''completely'':101B ''conclusion'':215B ''conflict'':15B ''confront'':178B ''continent'':58B ''count'':117B ''counter-offensiv'':116B ''crisis'':2A,5B,35B,211B ''cult'':76B ''cyrodiil'':99B,149B ''daedr'':61B,121B ''daedric'':18B,180B ''dagon'':22B,80B,204B ''dawn'':73B ''defenseless'':151B ''despit'':131B ''destiny'':176B ''destroy'':102B ''devastation'':84B ''devot'':77B ''emerg'':62B,164B ''emperor'':42B ''empir'':135B ''ending'':209B ''fac'':93B ''facilitat'':69B ''fanatical'':75B ''figur'':168B ''forc'':142B,171B ''forcing'':122B ''from'':144B ''gat'':55B,67B,127B ''great'':10B ''heir'':159B ''heirs'':48B ''her'':174B ''his'':47B ''hist'':112B ''hord'':19B ''ignit'':37B ''in'':29B,63B,82B,98B,128B,182B ''into'':188B ''join'':170B ''key'':88B ''kings'':200B ''known'':7B ''kvatch'':97B ''largely'':150B ''last'':158B ''leading'':49B ''leaving'':148B ''lik'':146B ''maintain'':138B ''marking'':213B ''marsh'':107B ''martin'':155B,186B ''mehrun'':21B,79B,203B ''morrowind'':147B ''mount'':113B ''mythic'':72B ''numbers'':65B ''oblivion'':1A,4B,54B,207B ''of'':20B,26B,41B,53B,105B,160B,175B,191B,196B,199B,216B ''offensiv'':118B ''opening'':52B ''order'':139B ''overwhelming'':64B ''peopl'':25B ''pivotal'':167B ''pow'':195B ''provinc'':87B,145B ''region'':130B ''regions'':89B ''resulting'':81B ''sacrific'':185B ''septim'':44B,156B,162B ''sieg'':95B ''significant'':141B ''skyrim'':92B ''struggl'':136B ''successful'':115B ''such'':90B ''tamriel'':27B ''that'':129B ''the'':3B,9B,17B,24B,30B,34B,39B,51B,57B,71B,103B,111B,120B,126B,132B,134B,157B,161B,179B,194B,197B,210B,214B,217B ''them'':123B ''thes'':66B ''third'':218B ''this'':153B ''threat'':181B ''through'':59B ''throughout'':85B ''thus'':208B ''to'':50B,78B,124B,137B,177B,201B,206B ''transform'':187B ''turmoil'':133B ''upon'':109B ''uriel'':43B ''utilizing'':193B ''various'':86B ''vii'':45B ''was'':12B,36B,100B ''wer'':68B ''which'':60B ''whil'':96B ''widespr'':83B ''with'':140B,172B ''withdrawn'':143B ''year'':31B', 'scholar, daedra, daedric', '
The Oblivion Crisis, or Great Anguish, began in 3E 433 when the assassination of Emperor Uriel Septim VII and his heirs enabled the Daedric Prince Mehrunes Dagon to unleash his forces on Tamriel through Oblivion Gates. Facilitated by the Mythic Dawn cult, the Daedra wrought devastation, destroying cities like Kvatch and besieging regions such as Skyrim. In Black Marsh, the Argonians, guided by the Hist, successfully repelled the Daedric hordes. Amid the chaos, Martin Septim, the last of the Septim bloodline, sacrificed himself by becoming the Avatar of Akatosh, using the Amulet of Kings to banish Mehrunes Dagon and end the crisis, concluding the Third Era.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('almalexia', 'Almalexia, revered as Almalexia the Lover and Ayem, was one of the three God-Kings of the Tribunal, a pantheon that included Vivec and Sotha Sil. Known for her compassion and protective nature, she earned titles such as "Healing Mother," "Lady of Mercy," and "Mother Morrowind" among the Dunmer. Residing in the temple city of Mournhold, Almalexia was deeply connected with House Indoril and was well-regarded for her approachability, often walking among her people. As a mortal, she was married to Lord Indoril Nerevar, the First Councilor of Resdayn, and later became the Consort of Vivec after her husband''s death in the Battle of Red Mountain.

Following Nerevar''s demise, Almalexia and the other Tribunal members swore an oath to never exploit the divine power of the Heart of Lorkhan, a promise they eventually broke to achieve godhood. This transformation not only changed the Chimer into the Dunmer but also established the Tribunal as immortal protectors of the Dunmer people. However, the truth of their ascendance was obscured, portrayed instead as a result of their virtues and wisdom. Over time, Almalexia''s powers waned, particularly during her battles against Dagoth Ur. Eventually, she succumbed to madness, culminating in the murder of Sotha Sil and her own death in a failed attempt to eliminate the Nerevarine in 3E 427.', '''3e'':222B ''427'':223B ''achiev'':142B ''after'':101B ''against'':194B ''almalexi'':1A,2B,5B,59B,116B,186B ''also'':155B ''among'':49B,76B ''an'':123B ''and'':8B,26B,33B,46B,66B,94B,117B,182B,209B ''approachability'':73B ''as'':4B,40B,79B,159B,176B ''ascendanc'':171B ''attempt'':216B ''ayem'':9B ''battl'':108B,193B ''becam'':96B ''brok'':140B ''but'':154B ''chang'':148B ''chim'':150B ''city'':56B ''compassion'':32B ''connect'':62B ''consort'':98B ''councilor'':91B ''culminating'':202B ''dagoth'':195B ''death'':105B,212B ''deeply'':61B ''demis'':115B ''divin'':129B ''dunm'':51B,153B,164B ''during'':191B ''earn'':37B ''eliminat'':218B ''establish'':156B ''eventually'':139B,197B ''exploit'':127B ''fail'':215B ''first'':90B ''following'':112B ''for'':30B,71B ''god'':16B ''god-kings'':15B ''godhood'':143B ''healing'':41B ''heart'':133B ''her'':31B,72B,77B,102B,192B,210B ''hous'':64B ''howev'':166B ''husband'':103B ''immortal'':160B ''in'':53B,106B,203B,213B,221B ''includ'':24B ''indoril'':65B,87B ''inste'':175B ''into'':151B ''kings'':17B ''known'':29B ''lady'':43B ''lat'':95B ''lord'':86B ''lorkh'':135B ''lov'':7B ''madness'':201B ''marri'':84B ''members'':121B ''mercy'':45B ''morrowind'':48B ''mortal'':81B ''moth'':42B,47B ''mountain'':111B ''mournhold'':58B ''murd'':205B ''natur'':35B ''nerev'':88B,113B ''nerevarin'':220B ''nev'':126B ''not'':146B ''oath'':124B ''obscur'':173B ''of'':12B,18B,44B,57B,92B,99B,109B,131B,134B,162B,169B,179B,206B ''often'':74B ''one'':11B ''only'':147B ''other'':119B ''over'':184B ''own'':211B ''pantheon'':22B ''particularly'':190B ''peopl'':78B,165B ''portray'':174B ''pow'':130B ''powers'':188B ''promis'':137B ''protectiv'':34B ''protectors'':161B ''red'':110B ''regard'':70B ''resdayn'':93B ''residing'':52B ''result'':178B ''rever'':3B ''s'':104B,114B,187B ''she'':36B,82B,198B ''sil'':28B,208B ''soth'':27B,207B ''succumb'':199B ''such'':39B ''swor'':122B ''templ'':55B ''that'':23B ''the'':6B,13B,19B,50B,54B,89B,97B,107B,118B,128B,132B,149B,152B,157B,163B,167B,170B,180B,204B,219B ''they'':138B ''this'':144B ''thre'':14B ''tim'':185B ''titl'':38B ''to'':85B,125B,141B,200B,217B ''transformation'':145B ''tribunal'':20B,120B,158B ''truth'':168B ''ur'':196B ''virtu'':181B ''vivec'':25B,100B ''walking'':75B ''wan'':189B ''was'':10B,60B,67B,83B,172B ''well'':69B ''well-regard'':68B ''wisdom'':183B ''with'':63B', 'darkelf, daedra, daedric, scholar', 'Almalexia, also known as Almalexia the Lover and Ayem, was one of the three God-Kings of the Tribunal, along with Vivec and Sotha Sil. She was revered for her compassion and earned titles like "Healing Mother" and "Lady of Mercy." After her husband, Lord Indoril Nerevar, died, she became Vivec''s Consort. Almalexia and the Tribunal initially swore to never use the Heart of Lorkhan''s power, but they eventually did, achieving godhood and transforming the Chimer into the Dunmer. Over time, her powers faded, and she succumbed to madness, leading to the murder of Sotha Sil and her own death in a failed attempt to kill the Nerevarine in 3E 427.', '', '', 'figures');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('morag_tong', 'The Morag Tong, or "Foresters Guild" in Dark Elvish, is an ancient guild of assassins based in Morrowind, operating under the patronage of the Daedric Prince Mephala. Founded in the First Era, the Morag Tong gained prominence as a means to settle disputes within Morrowind''s Great Houses, preventing open warfare through sanctioned killings known as Honorable Writs of Execution. These writs allowed assassins to carry out legal executions with complete immunity, cementing their role in Dunmeri politics. Despite their lawful standing in Morrowind, their methods and allegiance to Mephala stirred controversy. Some legends suggest the guild originally worshipped Sithis and even influenced the formation of the Dark Brotherhood, a splinter group that rose to prominence outside of Morrowind. Over centuries, the Morag Tong oscillated between shadowy obscurity and political power, adapting to the changing tides of history.

The guild''s practices reflect Mephala''s principles of subtlety and precision, with their assassins employing shortblades, light armor, and archery as their tools of choice. Their deadly arsenal is meticulously crafted, blending functionality with intimidating designs that evoke chitinous creatures. The Morag Tong''s influence waned during periods of upheaval, such as after the assassination of the Akaviri Potentate Versidue-Shaie, but they resurfaced in times of need, maintaining their ancient role as arbiters of Dunmeri vendettas. Despite their decline after the Red Year, the Morag Tong remains active, taking contracts on Solstheim and mainland Morrowind, and often clashing with their sworn enemies, the Dark Brotherhood. Bound by their strict code and enduring reverence for Mephala, the Morag Tong continues to embody the dark yet lawful traditions of Morrowind’s political and spiritual heritage.', '''activ'':231B ''adapting'':134B ''after'':194B,223B ''akaviri'':199B ''allegianc'':90B ''allow'':65B ''an'':13B ''ancient'':14B,213B ''and'':89B,103B,131B,151B,160B,236B,239B,254B,274B ''arbiters'':216B ''archery'':161B ''armor'':159B ''arsenal'':169B ''as'':40B,58B,162B,193B,215B ''assassination'':196B ''assassins'':17B,66B,155B ''bas'':18B ''betw'':128B ''blending'':173B ''bound'':249B ''brotherhood'':111B,248B ''but'':204B ''by'':250B ''carry'':68B ''cementing'':75B ''centuri'':123B ''changing'':137B ''chitinous'':180B ''choic'':166B ''clashing'':241B ''cod'':253B ''complet'':73B ''continu'':262B ''contracts'':233B ''controversy'':94B ''craft'':172B ''creatur'':181B ''daedric'':27B ''dark'':10B,110B,247B,266B ''deadly'':168B ''declin'':222B ''designs'':177B ''despit'':81B,220B ''disput'':45B ''dunmeri'':79B,218B ''during'':188B ''elvish'':11B ''embody'':264B ''employing'':156B ''enduring'':255B ''enemi'':245B ''even'':104B ''evok'':179B ''execution'':62B ''executions'':71B ''first'':33B ''for'':257B ''foresters'':7B ''formation'':107B ''found'':30B ''functionality'