'The Glenmoril Wyrd, or Glenmoril Coven, is a network of female-dominated Bretonic covens across Tamriel, known for their reverence of nature and association with Daedric Princes like Hircine. These covens, often led by beastfolk such as hagravens, practice unique rites like curing lycanthropy and vampirism and shape-shifting into animals. Originally linked to the Druids of Galen, they became reclusive protectors of the wilds. Their covens, spread across High Rock, Skyrim, and Bangkorai, are influenced by various Daedric Princes, creating both alliances and rivalries. They played key roles in events like the Bloodmoon Prophecy and the introduction of lycanthropy to the Companions of Whiterun. Despite their decline, the Glenmoril Wyrd remains a powerful and mysterious force tied to the natural and supernatural realms.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('great_war', 'The Great War, also known as the First War Against the Empire by the Thalmor, was a significant conflict that occurred from 4E 171 to 175 between the Third Empire and the Third Aldmeri Dominion. The war began with a Thalmor invasion of Imperial territory, motivated by the desire to assert dominance following the destabilization of Summerset after the Oblivion Crisis. The Thalmor seized control of the Summerset Isles in 4E 22 and later annexed Valenwood and Elsweyr, further consolidating their power.

The conflict escalated after the Thalmor demanded tributes, territorial concessions, and the disbandment of the Blades, along with a ban on the worship of Talos. When the Emperor rejected these demands, the Thalmor initiated hostilities, leading to the capture of the Imperial City. Although the Empire managed to reclaim the city, the war ended with the signing of the White-Gold Concordat, a peace treaty that imposed harsh terms on the Empire, including the suppression of Talos worship and the cession of large portions of southern Hammerfell. This war marked a pivotal moment in Tamriel''s history, highlighting the decline of Imperial power and the rise of the Aldmeri Dominion.', '''171'':26B ''175'':28B ''22'':74B ''4e'':25B,73B ''after'':60B,88B ''against'':12B ''aldmeri'':36B,194B ''along'':101B ''also'':6B ''although'':128B ''and'':33B,75B,79B,95B,164B,189B ''annex'':77B ''as'':8B ''assert'':53B ''ban'':104B ''beg'':40B ''betw'':29B ''blad'':100B ''by'':15B,49B ''captur'':123B ''cession'':166B ''city'':127B,135B ''concessions'':94B ''concordat'':147B ''conflict'':21B,86B ''consolidating'':82B ''control'':67B ''crisis'':63B ''declin'':185B ''demand'':91B ''demands'':115B ''desir'':51B ''destabilization'':57B ''disbandment'':97B ''dominanc'':54B ''dominion'':37B,195B ''elsweyr'':80B ''emperor'':112B ''empir'':14B,32B,130B,157B ''ended'':138B ''escalat'':87B ''first'':10B ''following'':55B ''from'':24B ''furth'':81B ''gold'':146B ''great'':1A,4B ''hammerfell'':172B ''harsh'':153B ''highlighting'':183B ''history'':182B ''hostiliti'':119B ''imperial'':46B,126B,187B ''impos'':152B ''in'':72B,179B ''including'':158B ''initiat'':118B ''invasion'':44B ''isles'':71B ''known'':7B ''larg'':168B ''lat'':76B ''leading'':120B ''manag'':131B ''mark'':175B ''moment'':178B ''motivat'':48B ''oblivion'':62B ''occurr'':23B ''of'':45B,58B,68B,98B,108B,124B,142B,161B,167B,170B,186B,192B ''on'':105B,155B ''peac'':149B ''pivotal'':177B ''portions'':169B ''pow'':84B,188B ''reclaim'':133B ''reject'':113B ''ris'':191B ''s'':181B ''seiz'':66B ''significant'':20B ''signing'':141B ''southern'':171B ''summerset'':59B,70B ''suppression'':160B ''tal'':109B,162B ''tamriel'':180B ''terms'':154B ''territorial'':93B ''territory'':47B ''thalmor'':17B,43B,65B,90B,117B ''that'':22B,151B ''the'':3B,9B,13B,16B,30B,34B,38B,50B,56B,61B,64B,69B,83B,85B,89B,96B,99B,106B,111B,116B,122B,125B,129B,134B,136B,140B,143B,156B,159B,165B,184B,190B,193B ''thes'':114B ''third'':31B,35B ''this'':173B ''to'':27B,52B,121B,132B ''treaty'':150B ''tribut'':92B ''valenwood'':78B ''war'':2A,5B,11B,39B,137B,174B ''was'':18B ''when'':110B ''whit'':145B ''white-gold'':144B ''with'':41B,102B,139B ''worship'':107B,163B', 'scholar, noble, legion, stormcloak', '
The Great War (4E 171–175) was a major conflict between the Third Empire and the Third Aldmeri Dominion. Sparked by the Thalmor''s demands for tributes, territorial concessions, and the suppression of Talos worship, the Empire''s rejection led to a Thalmor invasion and the capture of the Imperial City. Though the Empire reclaimed the city, the war concluded with the White-Gold Concordat, a treaty imposing harsh terms, including banning Talos worship and ceding southern Hammerfell. The war signified the Empire''s decline and the Aldmeri Dominion''s rise as a dominant power in Tamriel.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('ayleids', 'The Ayleids, also known as the Heartland High Elves, Wild Elves, or Saliache, were the first race to establish an empire in Tamriel, ruling over what is now modern-day Cyrodiil for countless generations. Their physical appearance was characterized by a thin, lean build, pointed ears, and angular facial features. They possessed a complexion that ranged from light tan to dark bronze, with eye colors varying from white to turquoise.

Fluent in Ayleidoon, their language, the Ayleids created the Imperial City and were responsible for constructing the White-Gold Tower, originally known as the "Temple of the Ancestors," in emulation of the Adamantine Tower. The Ayleid Empire ultimately collapsed during the early First Era, following the Alessian Slave Rebellion. While legends suggest that remnants of the Ayleids may still exist in the wilds of Tamriel, documented sightings have been extremely rare, with none reported in the Third or Fourth Eras.', '''adamantin'':106B ''alessi'':120B ''also'':4B ''an'':21B ''ancestors'':101B ''and'':49B,84B ''angul'':50B ''appearanc'':39B ''as'':6B,96B ''ayle'':109B ''ayleidoon'':75B ''ayleids'':1A,3B,79B,130B ''been'':142B ''bronz'':64B ''build'':46B ''by'':42B ''characteriz'':41B ''city'':83B ''collaps'':112B ''colors'':67B ''complexion'':56B ''constructing'':88B ''countless'':35B ''creat'':80B ''cyrodiil'':33B ''dark'':63B ''day'':32B ''document'':139B ''during'':113B ''early'':115B ''ears'':48B ''elves'':10B,12B ''empir'':22B,110B ''emulation'':103B ''establish'':20B ''exist'':133B ''extremely'':143B ''eye'':66B ''facial'':51B ''featur'':52B ''first'':17B,116B ''fluent'':73B ''following'':118B ''for'':34B,87B ''fourth'':152B ''from'':59B,69B ''generations'':36B ''gold'':92B ''hav'':141B ''heartland'':8B ''high'':9B ''imperial'':82B ''in'':23B,74B,102B,134B,148B ''is'':28B ''known'':5B,95B ''languag'':77B ''lean'':45B ''legends'':124B ''light'':60B ''may'':131B ''modern'':31B ''modern-day'':30B ''non'':146B ''now'':29B ''of'':99B,104B,128B,137B ''or'':13B,151B ''originally'':94B ''over'':26B ''physical'':38B ''point'':47B ''possess'':54B ''rac'':18B ''rang'':58B ''rar'':144B ''rebellion'':122B ''remnants'':127B ''report'':147B ''respons'':86B ''ruling'':25B ''saliach'':14B ''sightings'':140B ''slav'':121B ''still'':132B ''suggest'':125B ''tamriel'':24B,138B ''tan'':61B ''templ'':98B ''that'':57B,126B ''the'':2B,7B,16B,37B,76B,78B,81B,89B,97B,100B,105B,108B,114B,119B,129B,135B,149B ''they'':53B ''thin'':44B ''third'':150B ''to'':19B,62B,71B ''tow'':93B,107B ''turquois'':72B ''ultimately'':111B ''varying'':68B ''was'':40B ''wer'':15B,85B ''what'':27B ''whil'':123B ''whit'':70B,91B ''white-gold'':90B ''wild'':11B ''wilds'':136B ''with'':65B,145B', ' highelf, imperial, scholar', '
The Ayleids, or Heartland High Elves, were the first to establish an empire in Tamriel, ruling modern-day Cyrodiil for generations. Known for their lean build, bronze-toned skin, and varied eye colors, they spoke Ayleidoon and built the Imperial City and the White-Gold Tower, inspired by the Adamantine Tower. Their empire fell in the early First Era after the Alessian Slave Rebellion. While legends suggest some Ayleids may survive in Tamriel''s wilds, no confirmed sightings have been reported since the Third Era.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('planemeld', 'The Planemeld was a significant Daedric invasion of Tamriel that occurred in 2E 582 during the Interregnum. Orchestrated by the Daedric Prince Molag Bal, the invasion aimed to pull Nirn into his realm of Coldharbour using Dark Anchors—massive Daedric machines designed for interplanar manipulation. These Dark Anchors created rifts of darkness across the continent, directly linking Tamriel to Coldharbour.', '''2e'':14B ''582'':15B ''across'':54B ''aim'':28B ''anchors'':39B,49B ''bal'':25B ''by'':20B ''coldharbour'':36B,61B ''continent'':56B ''creat'':50B ''daedric'':7B,22B,41B ''dark'':38B,48B ''darkness'':53B ''design'':43B ''directly'':57B ''during'':16B ''for'':44B ''his'':33B ''in'':13B ''interplan'':45B ''interregnum'':18B ''into'':32B ''invasion'':8B,27B ''linking'':58B ''machin'':42B ''manipulation'':46B ''massiv'':40B ''molag'':24B ''nirn'':31B ''occurr'':12B ''of'':9B,35B,52B ''orchestrat'':19B ''planemeld'':1A,3B ''princ'':23B ''pull'':30B ''realm'':34B ''rifts'':51B ''significant'':6B ''tamriel'':10B,59B ''that'':11B ''the'':2B,17B,21B,26B,55B ''thes'':47B ''to'':29B,60B ''using'':37B ''was'':4B', 'daedra, scholar', '
The Planemeld was a Daedric invasion of Tamriel in 2E 582, led by Molag Bal during the Interregnum. His goal was to merge Nirn with his realm of Coldharbour using Dark Anchors—massive machines that created rifts of darkness, linking Tamriel directly to Coldharbour. This catastrophic event threatened the continent with destruction and domination by the Daedric Prince.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('alessian_empire', 'The Alessian Empire, established in 1E 243, was founded by former Nede slaves who revolted against their Ayleid oppressors during the Alessian Slave Rebellion. Centered in the Imperial City, the empire was marked by its founding figure, Queen Alessia, who received the Amulet of Kings from Akatosh, a gift that promised to protect Tamriel from the Daedra as long as the Dragonfires remained lit. Over its long history, the empire expanded to include parts of Cyrodiil, Skyrim, and High Rock, enduring for over two thousand years and heavily influenced by the religious teachings of the Alessian Order.

The empire''s governance transitioned from an absolute monarchy to a theocratic rule as the power of the Alessian Order grew, especially after its doctrines were adopted in 1E 361. However, internal strife and the rising influence of the Colovian Estates ultimately led to the empire''s decline. By 1E 2331, the Alessian Empire had fragmented, resulting in the dissolution of the Order and the establishment of a puppet government in Cyrodiil, setting the stage for the eventual rise of the Second Empire under Reman I in 1E 2703.', '''1e'':8B,128B,149B,187B ''2331'':150B ''243'':9B ''2703'':188B ''361'':129B ''absolut'':107B ''adopt'':126B ''after'':122B ''against'':18B ''akatosh'':49B ''alessi'':1A,4B,24B,41B,98B,118B,152B ''amulet'':45B ''an'':106B ''and'':80B,89B,133B,163B ''as'':60B,62B,113B ''ayle'':20B ''by'':12B,36B,92B,148B ''center'':27B ''city'':31B ''colovi'':139B ''cyrodiil'':78B,171B ''daedr'':59B ''declin'':147B ''dissolution'':159B ''doctrin'':124B ''dragonfir'':64B ''during'':22B ''empir'':2A,5B,33B,72B,101B,145B,153B,182B ''enduring'':83B ''especially'':121B ''establish'':6B ''establishment'':165B ''estat'':140B ''eventual'':177B ''expand'':73B ''figur'':39B ''for'':84B,175B ''form'':13B ''found'':11B ''founding'':38B ''fragment'':155B ''from'':48B,57B,105B ''gift'':51B ''governanc'':103B ''government'':169B ''grew'':120B ''had'':154B ''heavily'':90B ''high'':81B ''history'':70B ''howev'':130B ''i'':185B ''imperial'':30B ''in'':7B,28B,127B,157B,170B,186B ''includ'':75B ''influenc'':91B,136B ''internal'':131B ''its'':37B,68B,123B ''kings'':47B ''led'':142B ''lit'':66B ''long'':61B,69B ''mark'':35B ''monarchy'':108B ''ned'':14B ''of'':46B,77B,96B,116B,137B,160B,166B,179B ''oppressors'':21B ''order'':99B,119B,162B ''over'':67B,85B ''parts'':76B ''pow'':115B ''promis'':53B ''protect'':55B ''puppet'':168B ''que'':40B ''rebellion'':26B ''receiv'':43B ''religious'':94B ''rem'':184B ''remain'':65B ''resulting'':156B ''revolt'':17B ''ris'':178B ''rising'':135B ''rock'':82B ''rul'':112B ''s'':102B,146B ''second'':181B ''setting'':172B ''skyrim'':79B ''slav'':15B,25B ''stag'':174B ''strif'':132B ''tamriel'':56B ''teachings'':95B ''that'':52B ''the'':3B,19B,23B,29B,32B,44B,58B,63B,71B,93B,97B,100B,114B,117B,134B,138B,144B,151B,158B,161B,164B,173B,176B,180B ''theocratic'':111B ''thousand'':87B ''to'':54B,74B,109B,143B ''transition'':104B ''two'':86B ''ultimately'':141B ''under'':183B ''was'':10B,34B ''wer'':125B ''who'':16B,42B ''years'':88B', 'imperial, highelf, scholar', '
The Alessian Empire, founded in 1E 243 after the Alessian Slave Rebellion, was established by freed Nede slaves led by Queen Alessia, who received the Amulet of Kings from Akatosh. Centered in the Imperial City, it expanded to include parts of Cyrodiil, Skyrim, and High Rock, lasting over two thousand years. The empire shifted from monarchy to theocratic rule under the influence of the Alessian Order, whose doctrines shaped its governance from 1E 361. Internal conflicts and the rise of the Colovian Estates led to its fragmentation by 1E 2331, paving the way for the Second Empire under Reman I in 1E 2703.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('colovian', 'Colovia, also known as the Colovian West or Old Colovia, constitutes the western half of the province of Cyrodiil. Renowned for its strong-willed and industrious inhabitants, Colovia has a rich martial tradition, with many citizens serving in the Imperial Legion. The region is characterized by its rugged landscapes, which include hilly terrains along the Gold Road, dense forests in the Great Forest, and expansive grasslands within the Imperial Reserve, a well-known area for hunting.

The Colovians are known for their self-sufficiency and craftsmanship, particularly in timber, which is highly sought after for construction and weaponry. While most Colovians reside in major cities such as Anvil, Chorrol, Kvatch, and Skingrad, the nobility often inhabit private estates near the Gold Coast. Despite shifts in territorial borders over the eras, Colovia remains a vital part of Cyrodiil, blending a fierce frontier spirit with a deep appreciation for their agricultural and natural resources.', '''after'':98B ''agricultural'':153B ''along'':56B ''also'':3B ''and'':27B,66B,89B,101B,115B,154B ''anvil'':112B ''appreciation'':150B ''are'':77B,82B ''as'':5B,111B ''blending'':142B ''borders'':131B ''by'':48B ''characteriz'':47B ''chorrol'':113B ''citi'':109B ''citizens'':38B ''coast'':126B ''colovi'':1A,2B,7B,11B,30B,135B ''colovians'':81B,105B ''constitut'':12B ''construction'':100B ''craftsmanship'':90B ''cyrodiil'':20B,141B ''deep'':149B ''dens'':60B ''despit'':127B ''estat'':122B ''expansiv'':67B ''fierc'':144B ''for'':22B,78B,84B,99B,151B ''forest'':65B ''forests'':61B ''fronti'':145B ''gold'':58B,125B ''grasslands'':68B ''great'':64B ''half'':15B ''highly'':96B ''hilly'':54B ''hunting'':79B ''imperial'':42B,71B ''in'':40B,62B,92B,107B,129B ''includ'':53B ''industrious'':28B ''inhabit'':120B ''inhabitants'':29B ''is'':46B,95B ''its'':23B,49B ''known'':4B,76B,83B ''kvatch'':114B ''landscap'':51B ''legion'':43B ''major'':108B ''many'':37B ''martial'':34B ''most'':104B ''natural'':155B ''near'':123B ''nobility'':118B ''of'':16B,19B,140B ''often'':119B ''old'':10B ''or'':9B ''over'':132B ''part'':139B ''particularly'':91B ''privat'':121B ''provinc'':18B ''region'':45B ''remains'':136B ''renown'':21B ''reserv'':72B ''resid'':106B ''resourc'':156B ''rich'':33B ''road'':59B ''rugg'':50B ''self'':87B ''self-sufficiency'':86B ''serving'':39B ''shifts'':128B ''skingr'':116B ''sought'':97B ''spirit'':146B ''strong'':25B ''strong-will'':24B ''such'':110B ''sufficiency'':88B ''terrains'':55B ''territorial'':130B ''the'':6B,13B,17B,41B,44B,57B,63B,70B,80B,85B,117B,124B,133B,152B ''timb'':93B ''tradition'':35B ''vital'':138B ''weaponry'':102B ''well'':75B ''well-known'':74B ''west'':8B ''western'':14B ''which'':52B,94B ''whil'':103B ''will'':26B ''with'':36B,147B ''within'':69B', 'imperial, nord, scholar', 'Colovia, or the Colovian West, forms the western half of Cyrodiil, known for its rugged landscapes of hills, forests, and grasslands. Its strong-willed and industrious people uphold a rich martial tradition, with many serving in the Imperial Legion. Renowned for self-sufficiency, Colovians excel in timber craftsmanship for construction and weaponry. Key cities include Anvil, Chorrol, Kvatch, and Skingrad, while the nobility often reside on private estates along the Gold Coast. Colovia''s blend of frontier spirit and agricultural resourcefulness makes it a vital part of Cyrodiil''s identity.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('black_briar', 'The Black-Briars are a powerful and influential family faction based in Riften, Skyrim, with far-reaching control over the city''s affairs and beyond. Led by the ruthless Maven Black-Briar, the family dominates local politics, industry, and the criminal underworld. Maven, the matriarch, is known for her cunning and willingness to use whatever means necessary to expand her wealth and influence, including leveraging the Thieves Guild, the Dark Brotherhood, and even the Thalmor. Through manipulation and bribery, she keeps Riften''s guards and Jarl Laila Law-Giver in her pocket, ensuring the family’s dominance in the region. The family’s primary business, Black-Briar Meadery, is a significant source of their power, with aspirations of expanding its reach across Tamriel.

The Black-Briar family consists of four main members, each with their own ambitions and flaws. Hemming Black-Briar, Maven''s loyal son, is pompous and ambitious, aiming to spread the family''s meadery empire far and wide. Sibbi Black-Briar, the disgraced son, tarnishes the family name with his crimes, including murder and adultery, and currently resides in a luxurious prison cell in Mistveil Watch. Ingun Black-Briar, the eccentric daughter, distances herself from the family’s scheming, focusing instead on her passion for alchemy, fascinated by the delicate balance between life and death. Together, the Black-Briars represent a dark yet formidable force within Skyrim, their alliances and cunning ensuring their influence extends well beyond the walls of Riften.', '''across'':129B ''adultery'':188B ''affairs'':27B ''aiming'':160B ''alchemy'':220B ''allianc'':244B ''ambitions'':145B ''ambitious'':159B ''and'':10B,28B,44B,56B,67B,77B,83B,90B,146B,158B,169B,187B,189B,228B,245B ''are'':7B ''aspirations'':124B ''balanc'':225B ''bas'':14B ''betw'':226B ''beyond'':29B,252B ''black'':1A,5B,36B,113B,133B,150B,173B,202B,233B ''black-bri'':35B,112B,132B,149B,172B,201B ''black-briars'':4B,232B ''bri'':2A,37B,114B,134B,151B,174B,203B ''briars'':6B,234B ''bribery'':84B ''brotherhood'':76B ''business'':111B ''by'':31B,222B ''cell'':196B ''city'':25B ''consists'':136B ''control'':22B ''crim'':184B ''criminal'':46B ''cunning'':55B,246B ''currently'':190B ''dark'':75B,237B ''daught'':206B ''death'':229B ''delicat'':224B ''disgrac'':176B ''distanc'':207B ''dominanc'':103B ''dominat'':40B ''each'':141B ''eccentric'':205B ''empir'':167B ''ensuring'':99B,247B ''even'':78B ''expand'':64B ''expanding'':126B ''extends'':250B ''faction'':13B ''family'':12B,39B,101B,108B,135B,164B,180B,211B ''far'':20B,168B ''far-reaching'':19B ''fascinat'':221B ''flaws'':147B ''focusing'':214B ''for'':53B,219B ''forc'':240B ''formid'':239B ''four'':138B ''from'':209B ''giv'':95B ''guards'':89B ''guild'':73B ''hemming'':148B ''her'':54B,65B,97B,217B ''herself'':208B ''his'':183B ''in'':15B,96B,104B,192B,197B ''including'':69B,185B ''industry'':43B ''influenc'':68B,249B ''influential'':11B ''ingun'':200B ''inste'':215B ''is'':51B,116B,156B ''its'':127B ''jarl'':91B ''keeps'':86B ''known'':52B ''lail'':92B ''law'':94B ''law-giv'':93B ''led'':30B ''leveraging'':70B ''lif'':227B ''local'':41B ''loyal'':154B ''luxurious'':194B ''main'':139B ''manipulation'':82B ''matriarch'':50B ''mav'':34B,48B,152B ''meadery'':115B,166B ''means'':61B ''members'':140B ''mistveil'':198B ''murd'':186B ''nam'':181B ''necessary'':62B ''of'':120B,125B,137B,255B ''on'':216B ''over'':23B ''own'':144B ''passion'':218B ''pocket'':98B ''politics'':42B ''pompous'':157B ''pow'':122B ''powerful'':9B ''primary'':110B ''prison'':195B ''reach'':128B ''reaching'':21B ''region'':106B ''represent'':235B ''resid'':191B ''rift'':16B,87B,256B ''ruthless'':33B ''s'':26B,88B,102B,109B,153B,165B,212B ''scheming'':213B ''she'':85B ''sibbi'':171B ''significant'':118B ''skyrim'':17B,242B ''sourc'':119B ''spre'':162B ''tamriel'':130B ''tarnish'':178B ''thalmor'':80B ''the'':3B,24B,32B,38B,45B,49B,71B,74B,79B,100B,105B,107B,121B,131B,143B,163B,175B,179B,204B,210B,223B,231B,243B,248B,253B ''thiev'':72B ''through'':81B ''to'':58B,63B,161B ''togeth'':230B ''underworld'':47B ''use'':59B ''walls'':254B ''watch'':199B ''wealth'':66B ''well'':251B ''whatev'':60B ''wid'':170B ''willingness'':57B ''with'':18B,123B,142B,182B ''within'':241B ''yet'':238B', 'rift, innkeeper, noble', 'The Black-Briars are a powerful family in Riften, Skyrim, led by the ruthless Maven Black-Briar, who controls local politics, industry, and the criminal underworld. Through manipulation, bribery, and alliances with groups like the Thieves Guild and the Dark Brotherhood, Maven ensures the family''s dominance, particularly through their profitable business, Black-Briar Meadery. The family includes Maven''s loyal son Hemming, the disgraced Sibbi, and the eccentric Ingun, who focuses on alchemy rather than the family''s schemes. The Black-Briars are a formidable force in Skyrim, with their influence extending far beyond Riften.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('stallions_potion', 'The Stallion''s Potion is a unique potion that boosts stamina for an extended period and is often associated with physical endurance.', '''an'':15B ''and'':18B ''associat'':21B ''boosts'':12B ''enduranc'':24B ''extend'':16B ''for'':14B ''is'':7B,19B ''often'':20B ''period'':17B ''physical'':23B ''potion'':2A,6B,10B ''s'':5B ''stallion'':4B ''stallions'':1A ''stamin'':13B ''that'':11B ''the'':3B ''uniqu'':9B ''with'':22B', 'bothela', 'You assume it must be endurance-enhancing by the name but know nothing else about its origins. You have heard it most often in association with Markarth.', '', '', 'items');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('nedes', 'The Nedes, also referred to as the Nedic peoples, were a group of human races that inhabited much of Tamriel during the Merethic and First Eras. Their society included the proto-Cyrodilians, ancestors of the Bretons, and aboriginal populations in regions such as Hammerfell and possibly Morrowind. The Duraki society within the Nedes, particularly in Hammerfell, practiced the Cult of the Stars, which focused on the worship and study of celestial constellations and beings known as the Celestials.

Over time, Nedic culture experienced significant decline as they assimilated into other cultures, ultimately leading to their near extinction. The Yokudan invasion of Hammerfell marked a pivotal moment in their history, resulting in the extermination of the Nedes in that region. The legacy of the Nedic peoples is reflected in the various cultures that evolved from their original societies across Tamriel.', '''aboriginal'':40B ''across'':140B ''also'':4B ''ancestors'':35B ''and'':25B,39B,47B,70B,75B ''as'':7B,45B,78B,88B ''assimilat'':90B ''beings'':76B ''bretons'':38B ''celestial'':73B ''celestials'':80B ''constellations'':74B ''cult'':61B ''cultur'':84B,93B,133B ''cyrodilians'':34B ''declin'':87B ''duraki'':51B ''during'':22B ''evolv'':135B ''experienc'':85B ''extermination'':115B ''extinction'':99B ''first'':26B ''focus'':66B ''from'':136B ''group'':13B ''hammerfell'':46B,58B,104B ''history'':111B ''hum'':15B ''in'':42B,57B,109B,113B,119B,130B ''includ'':30B ''inhabit'':18B ''into'':91B ''invasion'':102B ''is'':128B ''known'':77B ''leading'':95B ''legacy'':123B ''mark'':105B ''merethic'':24B ''moment'':108B ''morrowind'':49B ''much'':19B ''near'':98B ''ned'':1A,3B,55B,118B ''nedic'':9B,83B,126B ''of'':14B,20B,36B,62B,72B,103B,116B,124B ''on'':67B ''original'':138B ''other'':92B ''over'':81B ''particularly'':56B ''peopl'':10B,127B ''pivotal'':107B ''populations'':41B ''possibly'':48B ''practic'':59B ''prot'':33B ''proto-cyrodilians'':32B ''rac'':16B ''referr'':5B ''reflect'':129B ''region'':121B ''regions'':43B ''resulting'':112B ''significant'':86B ''societi'':139B ''society'':29B,52B ''stars'':64B ''study'':71B ''such'':44B ''tamriel'':21B,141B ''that'':17B,120B,134B ''the'':2B,8B,23B,28B,31B,37B,50B,54B,60B,63B,68B,79B,97B,100B,110B,114B,117B,122B,125B,131B,137B ''they'':89B ''tim'':82B ''to'':6B,96B ''ultimately'':94B ''various'':132B ''wer'':11B ''which'':65B ''within'':53B ''worship'':69B ''yokud'':101B', 'imperial, nord, scholar', 'The Nedes, or Nedic peoples, were early human races inhabiting Tamriel during the Merethic and First Eras. They included proto-Cyrodilians, ancestors of the Bretons, and aboriginal groups in regions like Hammerfell. Notably, the Duraki Nedes of Hammerfell worshipped celestial beings through the Cult of the Stars. Over time, Nedic culture declined due to assimilation and conflicts, with the Yokudan invasion of Hammerfell leading to their extermination there. The Nedes'' legacy endures in the cultures that evolved from their societies across Tamriel.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('aldmeri', 'The Aldmer, meaning "First Folk" or "Elder Folk," are the original inhabitants of Tamriel, having settled in Summerset Isle and much of the mainland during the Merethic Era. They are regarded as the first technologically advanced beings on Nirn, although this claim is sometimes contested. The Aldmer are said to have originated from a mythical continent known as Aldmeris, though this is widely debated among scholars. As they spread across Tamriel, they evolved into various types of elves, leading to the distinct groups known today, including the Altmer, Bosmer, Dwemer, Chimer, Ayleids, Snow Elves, Maormer, and Orcs.

Although specific details of Aldmer culture remain elusive, they were known for their chivalric high culture and musical traditions. Notable figures from Aldmer history include High Lord Torinaan, who established the Summerset Isles, and Topal the Pilot, who explored much of Tamriel. The modern Altmer are believed to closely resemble their Aldmer ancestors and strive to emulate their ways, even going so far as to selectively breed for traits associated with the original Aldmer. The term "Aldmeri" is often used to refer collectively to all elves or to denote members of the Aldmeri community.', '''across'':71B ''advanc'':37B ''aldmer'':3B,48B,103B,121B,150B,172B ''aldmeri'':1A,175B,191B ''aldmeris'':60B ''all'':183B ''although'':41B,99B ''altmer'':89B,143B ''among'':66B ''ancestors'':151B ''and'':21B,97B,115B,132B,152B ''are'':10B,31B,49B,144B ''as'':33B,59B,68B,162B ''associat'':168B ''ayleids'':93B ''beings'':38B ''believ'':145B ''bosm'':90B ''bre'':165B ''chim'':92B ''chivalric'':112B ''claim'':43B ''closely'':147B ''collectively'':181B ''community'':192B ''contest'':46B ''continent'':57B ''cultur'':104B,114B ''debat'':65B ''denot'':187B ''details'':101B ''distinct'':83B ''during'':26B ''dwem'':91B ''elder'':8B ''elusiv'':106B ''elves'':79B,95B,184B ''emulat'':155B ''establish'':128B ''even'':158B ''evolv'':74B ''explor'':137B ''far'':161B ''figur'':119B ''first'':5B,35B ''folk'':6B,9B ''for'':110B,166B ''from'':54B,120B ''going'':159B ''groups'':84B ''hav'':52B ''having'':16B ''high'':113B,124B ''history'':122B ''in'':18B ''includ'':123B ''including'':87B ''inhabitants'':13B ''into'':75B ''is'':44B,63B,176B ''isle'':20B ''isles'':131B ''known'':58B,85B,109B ''leading'':80B ''lord'':125B ''mainland'':25B ''maorm'':96B ''meaning'':4B ''members'':188B ''merethic'':28B ''modern'':142B ''much'':22B,138B ''musical'':116B ''mythical'':56B ''nirn'':40B ''notabl'':118B ''of'':14B,23B,78B,102B,139B,189B ''often'':177B ''on'':39B ''or'':7B,185B ''orcs'':98B ''original'':12B,171B ''originat'':53B ''pilot'':135B ''ref'':180B ''regard'':32B ''remain'':105B ''resembl'':148B ''said'':50B ''scholars'':67B ''selectively'':164B ''settl'':17B ''snow'':94B ''so'':160B ''sometim'':45B ''specific'':100B ''spre'':70B ''striv'':153B ''summerset'':19B,130B ''tamriel'':15B,72B,140B ''technologically'':36B ''term'':174B ''the'':2B,11B,24B,27B,34B,47B,82B,88B,111B,129B,134B,141B,149B,156B,170B,173B,190B ''they'':30B,69B,73B,107B ''this'':42B,62B ''though'':61B ''to'':51B,81B,146B,154B,163B,179B,182B,186B ''today'':86B ''topal'':133B ''torin'':126B ''traditions'':117B ''traits'':167B ''types'':77B ''used'':178B ''various'':76B ''ways'':157B ''wer'':108B ''who'':127B,136B ''widely'':64B ''with'':169B', 'highelf, scholar', 'The Aldmer, or "First Folk," were the original inhabitants of Tamriel, settling in Summerset Isle and beyond during the Merethic Era. Believed to have originated from the mythical Aldmeris, they are regarded as Nirn''s first advanced civilization, though this is debated. Over time, the Aldmer evolved into distinct elven groups, including the Altmer, Bosmer, Dwemer, Chimer, Ayleids, and others. Known for their high culture and musical traditions, figures like High Lord Torinaan and Topal the Pilot are central to their history. Modern Altmer closely emulate their Aldmer ancestors, and "Aldmeri" often collectively refers to all elves or their shared heritage.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('chimer', 'The Chimer, meaning "People of the North," were a group of Aldmeri tribes that rejected the customs of Summerset Isle and undertook a significant exodus to Morrowind. This migration was led by the prophet Veloth, who conveyed the teachings of Boethiah, the Prince of Plots, along with two other Daedra. Embracing these teachings, the Chimer developed a distinct culture known as the High Velothi culture, marked by dynamic ambition and a strong emphasis on ancestor worship.

Characteristically, the Chimer possessed a dull golden skin tone and bright yellow eyes. They were known as the Changed Ones due to a transformation that occurred later in their history, leading to their eventual identification as the Dunmer, or Dark Elves. Despite this transformation, some figures from the Tribunal, such as Almalexia and Vivec, are noted to have retained elements of their Chimer heritage in their appearances. The Chimer''s legacy is intertwined with their cultural shifts and religious practices that significantly influenced the history of Morrowind.', '''aldmeri'':13B ''almalexi'':129B ''along'':47B ''ambition'':70B ''ancestor'':76B ''and'':22B,71B,87B,130B,155B ''appearanc'':144B ''are'':132B ''as'':62B,94B,113B,128B ''boethiah'':42B ''bright'':88B ''by'':33B,68B ''chang'':96B ''characteristically'':78B ''chim'':1A,3B,56B,80B,140B,146B ''convey'':38B ''cultur'':60B,66B ''cultural'':153B ''customs'':18B ''daedr'':51B ''dark'':117B ''despit'':119B ''develop'':57B ''distinct'':59B ''due'':98B ''dull'':83B ''dunm'':115B ''dynamic'':69B ''elements'':137B ''elves'':118B ''embracing'':52B ''emphasis'':74B ''eventual'':111B ''exodus'':26B ''eyes'':90B ''figur'':123B ''from'':124B ''gold'':84B ''group'':11B ''hav'':135B ''heritag'':141B ''high'':64B ''history'':107B,162B ''identification'':112B ''in'':105B,142B ''influenc'':160B ''intertwin'':150B ''is'':149B ''isle'':21B ''known'':61B,93B ''lat'':104B ''leading'':108B ''led'':32B ''legacy'':148B ''mark'':67B ''meaning'':4B ''migration'':30B ''morrowind'':28B,164B ''north'':8B ''not'':133B ''occurr'':103B ''of'':6B,12B,19B,41B,45B,138B,163B ''on'':75B ''ones'':97B ''or'':116B ''other'':50B ''peopl'':5B ''plots'':46B ''possess'':81B ''practic'':157B ''princ'':44B ''prophet'':35B ''reject'':16B ''religious'':156B ''retain'':136B ''s'':147B ''shifts'':154B ''significant'':25B ''significantly'':159B ''skin'':85B ''som'':122B ''strong'':73B ''such'':127B ''summerset'':20B ''teachings'':40B,54B ''that'':15B,102B,158B ''the'':2B,7B,17B,34B,39B,43B,55B,63B,79B,95B,106B,110B,114B,125B,139B,143B,145B,152B,161B ''thes'':53B ''they'':91B ''this'':29B,120B ''to'':27B,99B,109B,134B ''ton'':86B ''transformation'':101B,121B ''trib'':14B ''tribunal'':126B ''two'':49B ''undertook'':23B ''veloth'':36B ''velothi'':65B ''vivec'':131B ''was'':31B ''wer'':9B,92B ''who'':37B ''with'':48B,151B ''worship'':77B ''yellow'':89B', 'darkelf, daedra, scholar', '
The Chimer, or "People of the North," were Aldmeri tribes who, led by the prophet Veloth, migrated to Morrowind, rejecting Summerset''s customs and embracing the teachings of Boethiah and other Daedra. Known for their golden skin and yellow eyes, they developed the High Velothi culture, emphasizing ambition and ancestor worship. Their transformation into the Dunmer, or Dark Elves, marked a pivotal shift in their history, though some Tribunal figures, like Almalexia and Vivec, retained Chimeric traits. The Chimer''s cultural and religious practices profoundly shaped Morrowind''s history and identity.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('dragon_break', 'A Dragon Break is a significant and complex phenomenon in which linear time is disrupted, becoming non-linear and challenging the understanding of mortals. The term "Dragon" references Akatosh, the Dragon God of Time, indicating the involvement of divine forces in these events. A Dragon Break represents a catastrophic disruption of the continuity of time and space, often occurring in response to events that render normal reality untenable. This chaos mirrors the primordial disorder of the Dawn Era and can vary in its impact on different regions of Tamriel.

', '''akatosh'':32B ''and'':9B,22B,59B,82B ''becoming'':18B ''break'':2A,5B,49B ''can'':83B ''catastrophic'':52B ''cha'':73B ''challenging'':23B ''complex'':10B ''continuity'':56B ''dawn'':80B ''different'':89B ''disord'':77B ''disrupt'':17B ''disruption'':53B ''divin'':42B ''dragon'':1A,4B,30B,34B,48B ''events'':46B,66B ''forc'':43B ''god'':35B ''impact'':87B ''in'':12B,44B,63B,85B ''indicating'':38B ''involvement'':40B ''is'':6B,16B ''its'':86B ''lin'':14B,21B ''mirrors'':74B ''mortals'':27B ''non'':20B ''non-lin'':19B ''normal'':69B ''occurring'':62B ''of'':26B,36B,41B,54B,57B,78B,91B ''often'':61B ''on'':88B ''phenomenon'':11B ''primordial'':76B ''reality'':70B ''referenc'':31B ''regions'':90B ''rend'':68B ''represents'':50B ''respons'':64B ''significant'':8B ''spac'':60B ''tamriel'':92B ''term'':29B ''that'':67B ''the'':24B,28B,33B,39B,55B,75B,79B ''thes'':45B ''this'':72B ''tim'':15B,37B,58B ''to'':65B ''understanding'':25B ''unten'':71B ''vary'':84B ''which'':13B', 'scholar, dragon, daedra, daedric', 'A Dragon Break is a phenomenon where linear time is disrupted, becoming non-linear and incomprehensible to mortals. Named after Akatosh, the Dragon God of Time, it signifies a divine disruption of reality, often triggered by events that challenge the normal flow of time and space. Resembling the chaos of the Dawn Era, its effects vary across Tamriel, creating a fractured and unpredictable timeline.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('sheogorath', '
Sheogorath is the Daedric Prince of Madness, chaos, and creativity, known for his unpredictable and often cruel sense of humor. He rules the Shivering Isles, a plane of Oblivion divided into Mania and Dementia, reflecting the dual aspects of madness. Sheogorath is notorious for his bizarre, sadistic, and whimsical behavior, often treating mortals and even other Daedric Princes as his playthings. His appearance is usually that of a well-dressed, eccentric man wielding a cane, which contrasts sharply with his unpredictable and dangerous nature. Legends tell that Sheogorath was once Jyggalag, the Daedric Prince of Order, but was cursed by the other Daedric Princes to embody the chaos he once despised. This curse led to the recurring Greymarch, during which Jyggalag would briefly reclaim his form before reverting back to Sheogorath.

Sheogorath is revered and feared in many cultures, especially among the Khajiit, who call him the Skooma Cat, and the Dunmer, who regard him as one of the Four Corners of the House of Troubles. His influence is marked by a fondness for driving mortals into madness, either through direct interaction or by creating chaotic circumstances that lead to mental unraveling. He delights in playing cruel pranks, manipulating the minds of his followers, and challenging them with seemingly impossible tasks. Sheogorath’s iconic artifacts include the Wabbajack, a staff that can unpredictably transform its targets, and the Fork of Horripilation, a mundane-looking but cursed utensil he uses to torment mortals for his amusement.', '''among'':143B ''amusement'':248B ''and'':10B,16B,34B,49B,55B,84B,137B,152B,207B,229B ''appearanc'':64B ''artifacts'':217B ''as'':60B,158B ''aspects'':39B ''back'':131B ''befor'':129B ''behavior'':51B ''bizarr'':47B ''briefly'':125B ''but'':99B,238B ''by'':102B,173B,186B ''call'':147B ''can'':77B,224B ''cat'':151B ''cha'':9B,110B ''challenging'':208B ''chaotic'':188B ''circumstanc'':189B ''contrasts'':79B ''corners'':163B ''creating'':187B ''creativity'':11B ''cruel'':18B,199B ''cultur'':141B ''curs'':101B,115B,239B ''daedric'':5B,58B,95B,105B ''dangerous'':85B ''delights'':196B ''dementi'':35B ''despis'':113B ''direct'':183B ''divid'':31B ''dress'':72B ''driving'':177B ''dual'':38B ''dunm'':154B ''during'':121B ''eccentric'':73B ''eith'':181B ''embody'':108B ''especially'':142B ''even'':56B ''fear'':138B ''followers'':206B ''fondness'':175B ''for'':13B,45B,176B,246B ''fork'':231B ''form'':128B ''four'':162B ''greymarch'':120B ''him'':148B,157B ''his'':14B,46B,61B,63B,82B,127B,169B,205B,247B ''horripilation'':233B ''hous'':166B ''humor'':21B ''iconic'':216B ''imposs'':212B ''in'':139B,197B ''includ'':218B ''influenc'':170B ''interaction'':184B ''into'':32B,179B ''is'':3B,43B,65B,135B,171B ''isles'':26B ''its'':227B ''jyggalag'':93B,123B ''khajiit'':145B ''known'':12B ''lead'':191B ''led'':116B ''legends'':87B ''looking'':237B ''madness'':8B,41B,180B ''man'':74B ''mani'':33B ''manipulating'':201B ''many'':140B ''mark'':172B ''mental'':193B ''minds'':203B ''mortals'':54B,178B,245B ''mundan'':236B ''mundane-looking'':235B ''natur'':86B ''notorious'':44B ''oblivion'':30B ''of'':7B,20B,29B,40B,68B,97B,160B,164B,167B,204B,232B ''often'':17B,52B ''once'':92B,112B ''one'':159B ''or'':185B ''order'':98B ''other'':57B,104B ''plan'':28B ''playing'':198B ''playthings'':62B ''pranks'':200B ''princ'':6B,59B,96B,106B ''reclaim'':126B ''recurring'':119B ''reflecting'':36B ''regard'':156B ''rever'':136B ''reverting'':130B ''rul'':23B ''s'':215B ''sadistic'':48B ''seemingly'':211B ''sens'':19B ''sharply'':80B ''sheogorath'':1A,2B,42B,90B,133B,134B,214B ''shivering'':25B ''skoom'':150B ''staff'':222B ''targets'':228B ''tasks'':213B ''tell'':88B ''that'':67B,89B,190B,223B ''the'':4B,24B,37B,94B,103B,109B,118B,144B,149B,153B,161B,165B,202B,219B,230B ''them'':209B ''this'':114B ''through'':182B ''to'':107B,117B,132B,192B,243B ''torment'':244B ''transform'':226B ''treating'':53B ''troubl'':168B ''unpredict'':15B,83B ''unpredictably'':225B ''unraveling'':194B ''uses'':242B ''usually'':66B ''utensil'':240B ''wabbajack'':220B ''was'':91B,100B ''well'':71B ''well-dress'':70B ''which'':78B,122B ''whimsical'':50B ''who'':146B,155B ''wielding'':75B ''with'':81B,210B ''would'':124B', 'daedra, daedric, scholar', '
Sheogorath, the Daedric Prince of Madness, chaos, and creativity, rules the Shivering Isles, a realm divided into Mania and Dementia. Known for his unpredictable and cruel sense of humor, Sheogorath often treats mortals and even other Daedric Princes as playthings. Once Jyggalag, the Daedric Prince of Order, he was cursed to embody chaos, leading to the recurring Greymarch. Revered and feared across Tamriel, especially by the Khajiit and Dunmer, Sheogorath drives mortals to madness through his pranks and chaotic influence. His notable artifacts include the Wabbajack, a staff that unpredictably transforms targets, and the Fork of Horripilation, a cursed utensil used for torment.', '', '', 'figures');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('thrassian_plague', 'In 1E 2200, the Thrassian Plague emerged in Tamriel, unleashed by infected sea creatures along the coastlines. This devastating disease spread with alarming speed, resulting in the deaths of hundreds of thousands and leaving behind grotesque corpses. Symptoms included painful boils, brittle bones, seepage from the eyes and ears, and an insatiable thirst that drove the afflicted to madness. An Altmer Kinlord documented the Plague’s overwhelming nature, describing it as a force that permeated every living being and drop of water, leading to widespread despair.', '''1e'':4B ''2200'':5B ''afflict'':59B ''alarming'':25B ''along'':17B ''altmer'':63B ''an'':53B,62B ''and'':35B,50B,52B,81B ''as'':73B ''behind'':37B ''being'':80B ''boils'':43B ''bon'':45B ''brittl'':44B ''by'':13B ''coastlin'':19B ''corps'':39B ''creatur'':16B ''deaths'':30B ''describing'':71B ''desp'':88B ''devastating'':21B ''dis'':22B ''document'':65B ''drop'':82B ''drov'':57B ''ears'':51B ''emerg'':9B ''every'':78B ''eyes'':49B ''forc'':75B ''from'':47B ''grotesqu'':38B ''hundreds'':32B ''in'':3B,10B,28B ''includ'':41B ''infect'':14B ''insati'':54B ''it'':72B ''kinlord'':64B ''leading'':85B ''leaving'':36B ''living'':79B ''madness'':61B ''natur'':70B ''of'':31B,33B,83B ''overwhelming'':69B ''painful'':42B ''permeat'':77B ''plag'':2A,8B,67B ''resulting'':27B ''s'':68B ''seepag'':46B ''spe'':26B ''spre'':23B ''symptoms'':40B ''tamriel'':11B ''that'':56B,76B ''the'':6B,18B,29B,48B,58B,66B ''thirst'':55B ''this'':20B ''thousands'':34B ''thrassi'':1A,7B ''to'':60B,86B ''unleash'':12B ''wat'':84B ''widespr'':87B ''with'':24B', 'scholar', '
The Thrassian Plague, unleashed in 1E 2200 by infected sea creatures, was a catastrophic disease that spread rapidly across Tamriel, killing hundreds of thousands. Victims suffered from painful boils, brittle bones, eye and ear seepage, and a maddening thirst. An Altmer Kinlord described it as an all-encompassing force that tainted every living being and water source, leaving widespread devastation and despair in its wake.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('sload', 'The Sload, also known as Slugmen or slug-folk, are a race of slug-like beastfolk hailing from the Coral Kingdoms of Thras, located southwest of Tamriel. These semi-aquatic, corpulent beings can grow to significant sizes and are primarily known for their necromantic culture.

Described by the Khajiit explorer Ja''dasha as some of the most dangerous sea creatures, the Sload are often mentioned alongside krakens and sea serpents. Their notoriety escalated dramatically in 1E 2260 when they released the Thrassian Plague upon Tamriel, a catastrophic event that resulted in the deaths of over half the continent''s population, further solidifying their reputation as a significant threat in the region.', '''1e'':80B ''2260'':81B ''alongsid'':70B ''also'':4B ''and'':42B,72B ''aquatic'':34B ''are'':12B,43B,67B ''as'':6B,57B,109B ''beastfolk'':19B ''beings'':36B ''by'':51B ''can'':37B ''catastrophic'':91B ''continent'':102B ''coral'':23B ''corpulent'':35B ''creatur'':64B ''cultur'':49B ''dangerous'':62B ''dash'':56B ''deaths'':97B ''describ'':50B ''dramatically'':78B ''escalat'':77B ''event'':92B ''explor'':54B ''folk'':11B ''for'':46B ''from'':21B ''furth'':105B ''grow'':38B ''hailing'':20B ''half'':100B ''in'':79B,95B,113B ''ja'':55B ''khajiit'':53B ''kingdoms'':24B ''known'':5B,45B ''krakens'':71B ''lik'':18B ''locat'':27B ''mention'':69B ''most'':61B ''necromantic'':48B ''notoriety'':76B ''of'':15B,25B,29B,59B,98B ''often'':68B ''or'':8B ''over'':99B ''plag'':87B ''population'':104B ''primarily'':44B ''rac'':14B ''region'':115B ''releas'':84B ''reputation'':108B ''result'':94B ''s'':103B ''semi'':33B ''semi-aquatic'':32B ''serpents'':74B ''significant'':40B,111B ''siz'':41B ''slo'':1A,3B,66B ''slug'':10B,17B ''slug-folk'':9B ''slug-lik'':16B ''slugm'':7B ''solidifying'':106B ''som'':58B ''southwest'':28B ''tamriel'':30B,89B ''that'':93B ''the'':2B,22B,47B,52B,60B,65B,75B,85B,96B,101B,107B,114B ''thes'':31B ''they'':83B ''thras'':26B ''thrassi'':86B ''threat'':112B ''to'':39B ''upon'':88B ''when'':82B', 'scholar', '
The Sload, or Slugmen, are a slug-like race from the Coral Kingdoms of Thras, southwest of Tamriel. Known for their necromantic culture and semi-aquatic nature, they are infamous for their role in unleashing the Thrassian Plague in 1E 2260, which decimated over half of Tamriel''s population. Described as highly dangerous by explorers, they are often compared to krakens and sea serpents, cementing their reputation as a significant threat to the region.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('three_banners_war', 'The Three Banners War, also known as the Alliance War or the Great War, was a significant conflict that erupted across Tamriel during the Interregnum, marked by the fragmentation of the continent into three powerful alliances: the Daggerfall Covenant, the Ebonheart Pact, and the Aldmeri Dominion. This war arose from a series of crises and tensions in the years leading up to 2E 580, culminating in a struggle for control over the Imperial City and the Ruby Throne, as various factions sought to overthrow the corrupt Imperial regime.

The war primarily unfolded in Cyrodiil, which became the central battleground for these alliances. Each alliance engaged in fierce combat, causing widespread devastation to towns and cities, displacing countless civilians throughout the continent. Although the exact outcome of the war remains unclear, it resulted in significant shifts in power, ultimately leading to the dissolution of the three alliances and the Empire of Cyrodiil by the ninth century of the Second Era. Symbolically, the conflict is represented by a three-headed ouroboros, with each head denoting one of the alliances: an eagle for the Aldmeri Dominion, a lion for the Daggerfall Covenant, and a dragon for the Ebonheart Pact.', '''2e'':66B ''580'':67B ''across'':24B ''aldmeri'':48B,186B ''allianc'':12B,39B,105B,107B,149B,181B ''also'':8B ''although'':125B ''an'':182B ''and'':46B,58B,78B,117B,150B,194B ''aros'':52B ''as'':10B,82B ''banners'':2A,6B ''battleground'':102B ''becam'':99B ''by'':30B,155B,168B ''causing'':112B ''central'':101B ''century'':158B ''citi'':118B ''city'':77B ''civilians'':121B ''combat'':111B ''conflict'':21B,165B ''continent'':35B,124B ''control'':73B ''corrupt'':89B ''countless'':120B ''covenant'':42B,193B ''cris'':57B ''culminating'':68B ''cyrodiil'':97B,154B ''daggerfall'':41B,192B ''denoting'':177B ''devastation'':114B ''displacing'':119B ''dissolution'':145B ''dominion'':49B,187B ''dragon'':196B ''during'':26B ''each'':106B,175B ''eagl'':183B ''ebonheart'':44B,199B ''empir'':152B ''engag'':108B ''erupt'':23B ''exact'':127B ''factions'':84B ''fierc'':110B ''for'':72B,103B,184B,190B,197B ''fragmentation'':32B ''from'':53B ''great'':16B ''head'':172B,176B ''imperial'':76B,90B ''in'':60B,69B,96B,109B,136B,139B ''interregnum'':28B ''into'':36B ''is'':166B ''it'':134B ''known'':9B ''leading'':63B,142B ''lion'':189B ''mark'':29B ''ninth'':157B ''of'':33B,56B,129B,146B,153B,159B,179B ''one'':178B ''or'':14B ''ourobor'':173B ''outcom'':128B ''over'':74B ''overthrow'':87B ''pact'':45B,200B ''pow'':140B ''powerful'':38B ''primarily'':94B ''regim'':91B ''remains'':132B ''represent'':167B ''result'':135B ''ruby'':80B ''second'':161B ''seri'':55B ''shifts'':138B ''significant'':20B,137B ''sought'':85B ''struggl'':71B ''symbolically'':163B ''tamriel'':25B ''tensions'':59B ''that'':22B ''the'':4B,11B,15B,27B,31B,34B,40B,43B,47B,61B,75B,79B,88B,92B,100B,123B,126B,130B,144B,147B,151B,156B,160B,164B,180B,185B,191B,198B ''thes'':104B ''this'':50B ''thre'':1A,5B,37B,148B,171B ''three-head'':170B ''thron'':81B ''throughout'':122B ''to'':65B,86B,115B,143B ''towns'':116B ''ultimately'':141B ''uncle'':133B ''unfold'':95B ''up'':64B ''various'':83B ''war'':3A,7B,13B,17B,51B,93B,131B ''was'':18B ''which'':98B ''widespr'':113B ''with'':174B ''years'':62B', 'scholar', '
The Three Banners War, or Alliance War, occurred during the Interregnum, driven by the struggle for control over the Imperial City and the Ruby Throne. Beginning around 2E 580, it saw Tamriel divided into three alliances: the Aldmeri Dominion, Daggerfall Covenant, and Ebonheart Pact. Centered in Cyrodiil, the war caused widespread devastation and displaced many civilians. Although its exact resolution is unclear, it led to the collapse of the alliances and the Empire of Cyrodiil by the late Second Era. The war is symbolized by a three-headed ouroboros, with each head representing one alliance: an eagle (Aldmeri Dominion), a lion (Daggerfall Covenant), and a dragon (Ebonheart Pact).', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('ash_blight', 'In 2E 882, Dagoth Ur awoke from his slumber and unleashed the Ash Blight upon Morrowind, a severe weather phenomenon originating from Red Mountain. This volcanic event manifested as ash-heavy storms that carried a tainted, crimson dust, significantly affecting the health of those exposed to it. These blight storms became increasingly common and widespread, particularly by 3E 400, leading to a rise in soul sickness in areas near Red Mountain. House Redoran, struggling under the strain, mobilized volunteer forces to combat the encroaching effects of the Blight.

By 3E 427, the blight storms reached their peak intensity, contributing to the Vvardenfell Crisis. As the infection spread, boats from Vvardenfell were turned away from the mainland due to fears of contamination, isolating the island from the capital, Mournhold. Deformed beings known as "corprus men" emerged from the slopes of Red Mountain, bringing death and disease in their wake. The landscape became increasingly dangerous, as mortals and animals twisted by the Blight roamed the Ashlands, complicating travel and threatening the safety of those who ventured into the region.', '''2e'':4B ''3e'':61B,93B ''400'':62B ''427'':94B ''882'':5B ''affecting'':43B ''and'':12B,57B,147B,159B,170B ''animals'':160B ''are'':71B ''as'':31B,107B,135B,157B ''ash'':1A,15B,33B ''ash-heavy'':32B ''ashlands'':167B ''away'':116B ''awok'':8B ''becam'':54B,154B ''beings'':133B ''blight'':2A,16B,52B,91B,96B,164B ''boats'':111B ''bringing'':145B ''by'':60B,92B,162B ''capital'':130B ''carri'':37B ''combat'':85B ''common'':56B ''complicating'':168B ''contamination'':124B ''contributing'':102B ''corprus'':136B ''crimson'':40B ''crisis'':106B ''dagoth'':6B ''dangerous'':156B ''death'':146B ''deform'':132B ''dis'':148B ''due'':120B ''dust'':41B ''effects'':88B ''emerg'':138B ''encroaching'':87B ''event'':29B ''expos'':48B ''fears'':122B ''forc'':83B ''from'':9B,24B,112B,117B,128B,139B ''health'':45B ''heavy'':34B ''his'':10B ''hous'':75B ''in'':3B,67B,70B,149B ''increasingly'':55B,155B ''infection'':109B ''intensity'':101B ''into'':178B ''island'':127B ''isolating'':125B ''it'':50B ''known'':134B ''landscap'':153B ''leading'':63B ''mainland'':119B ''manifest'':30B ''men'':137B ''mobiliz'':81B ''morrowind'':18B ''mortals'':158B ''mountain'':26B,74B,144B ''mournhold'':131B ''near'':72B ''of'':46B,89B,123B,142B,174B ''originating'':23B ''particularly'':59B ''peak'':100B ''phenomenon'':22B ''reach'':98B ''red'':25B,73B,143B ''redor'':76B ''region'':180B ''ris'':66B ''roam'':165B ''safety'':173B ''sever'':20B ''sickness'':69B ''significantly'':42B ''slop'':141B ''slumb'':11B ''soul'':68B ''spre'':110B ''storms'':35B,53B,97B ''strain'':80B ''struggling'':77B ''taint'':39B ''that'':36B ''the'':14B,44B,79B,86B,90B,95B,99B,104B,108B,118B,126B,129B,140B,150B,152B,163B,166B,172B,179B ''thes'':51B ''this'':27B ''thos'':47B,175B ''threatening'':171B ''to'':49B,64B,84B,103B,121B ''travel'':169B ''turn'':115B ''twist'':161B ''under'':78B ''unleash'':13B ''upon'':17B ''ur'':7B ''ventur'':177B ''volcanic'':28B ''volunt'':82B ''vvardenfell'':105B,113B ''wak'':151B ''weath'':21B ''wer'':114B ''who'':176B ''widespr'':58B', 'darkelf, solstheim, scholar', '
In 2E 882, Dagoth Ur''s awakening triggered the Ash Blight in Morrowind, a volcanic phenomenon from Red Mountain that caused ash-heavy storms carrying crimson, tainted dust. These blight storms worsened by 3E 400, leading to widespread soul sickness near Red Mountain. House Redoran mobilized volunteers to combat the crisis, but by 3E 427, the storms peaked, isolating Vvardenfell as boats were barred from mainland Mournhold. Deformed "corprus men" and blight-twisted creatures emerged, making the Ashlands increasingly perilous and exacerbating the Vvardenfell Crisis.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('mede_dynasty', 'Following the fall of the Septim Dynasty at the end of the Third Era, High Chancellor Ocato became Potentate and led the Empire for over a decade until his assassination. This event triggered a tumultuous period known as the Stormcrown Interregnum, characterized by intense power struggles over the Ruby Throne. The interregnum concluded in 4E 22 when Titus Mede, a Colovian warlord, seized the Imperial City from the unpopular Nibenese battlemage Thules the Gibbering. Supported by Hierem, an influential figure from an old Nibenese family, Mede was crowned Emperor, establishing the Mede Dynasty.

Despite his efforts to restore order, the aftermath of the Oblivion Crisis had weakened Imperial control over its provinces, leading to widespread secession. Between 4E 1 and 4E 29, Black Marsh, Elsweyr, and Summerset Isle (renamed Alinor) broke away from the Empire. Morrowind faced devastation from the eruption of Red Mountain and an Argonian invasion, effectively ending its status as an Imperial province. The situation worsened under the reign of Titus Mede II, culminating in the Great War when the Aldmeri Dominion invaded in 4E 171. The conflict concluded in 4E 175 with the White-Gold Concordat, which forced Titus II to relinquish Hammerfell. By 4E 201, the Empire had shrunk to Cyrodiil, High Rock, and Skyrim, the latter of which was engulfed in two significant rebellions: the Stormcloak Rebellion and the Forsworn Rebellion.', '''1'':121B ''171'':181B ''175'':187B ''201'':203B ''22'':58B ''29'':124B ''4e'':57B,120B,123B,180B,186B,202B ''aftermath'':103B ''aldmeri'':176B ''alinor'':132B ''an'':80B,84B,148B,156B ''and'':22B,122B,128B,147B,212B,227B ''argoni'':149B ''as'':40B,155B ''assassination'':32B ''at'':10B ''away'':134B ''battlemag'':73B ''becam'':20B ''betw'':119B ''black'':125B ''brok'':133B ''by'':45B,78B,201B ''chancellor'':18B ''characteriz'':44B ''city'':68B ''colovi'':63B ''conclud'':55B,184B ''concordat'':193B ''conflict'':183B ''control'':111B ''crisis'':107B ''crown'':90B ''culminating'':169B ''cyrodiil'':209B ''decad'':29B ''despit'':96B ''devastation'':140B ''dominion'':177B ''dynasty'':2A,9B,95B ''effectively'':151B ''efforts'':98B ''elsweyr'':127B ''emperor'':91B ''empir'':25B,137B,205B ''end'':12B ''ending'':152B ''engulf'':219B ''eruption'':143B ''establishing'':92B ''event'':34B ''fac'':139B ''fall'':5B ''family'':87B ''figur'':82B ''following'':3B ''for'':26B ''forc'':195B ''forsworn'':229B ''from'':69B,83B,135B,141B ''gibbering'':76B ''gold'':192B ''great'':172B ''had'':108B,206B ''hammerfell'':200B ''hierem'':79B ''high'':17B,210B ''his'':31B,97B ''ii'':168B,197B ''imperial'':67B,110B,157B ''in'':56B,170B,179B,185B,220B ''influential'':81B ''intens'':46B ''interregnum'':43B,54B ''invad'':178B ''invasion'':150B ''isle'':130B ''its'':113B,153B ''known'':39B ''latt'':215B ''leading'':115B ''led'':23B ''marsh'':126B ''med'':1A,61B,88B,94B,167B ''morrowind'':138B ''mountain'':146B ''nibenes'':72B,86B ''oblivion'':106B ''ocat'':19B ''of'':6B,13B,104B,144B,165B,216B ''old'':85B ''order'':101B ''over'':27B,49B,112B ''period'':38B ''potentat'':21B ''pow'':47B ''provinc'':114B,158B ''rebellion'':226B,230B ''rebellions'':223B ''red'':145B ''reign'':164B ''relinquish'':199B ''renam'':131B ''restor'':100B ''rock'':211B ''ruby'':51B ''secession'':118B ''seiz'':65B ''septim'':8B ''shrunk'':207B ''significant'':222B ''situation'':160B ''skyrim'':213B ''status'':154B ''stormcloak'':225B ''stormcrown'':42B ''struggl'':48B ''summerset'':129B ''support'':77B ''the'':4B,7B,11B,14B,24B,41B,50B,53B,66B,70B,75B,93B,102B,105B,136B,142B,159B,163B,171B,175B,182B,189B,204B,214B,224B,228B ''third'':15B ''this'':33B ''thron'':52B ''thul'':74B ''titus'':60B,166B,196B ''to'':99B,116B,198B,208B ''trigger'':35B ''tumultuous'':37B ''two'':221B ''under'':162B ''unpopul'':71B ''until'':30B ''war'':173B ''warlord'':64B ''was'':89B,218B ''weaken'':109B ''when'':59B,174B ''which'':194B,217B ''whit'':191B ''white-gold'':190B ''widespr'':117B ''with'':188B ''worsen'':161B', 'imperial, scholar', '
After the fall of the Septim Dynasty at the end of the Third Era, High Chancellor Ocato became Potentate but was assassinated, sparking the Stormcrown Interregnum. This chaotic period ended in 4E 22 when Colovian warlord Titus Mede seized the Imperial City and established the Mede Dynasty. Despite efforts to restore order, the Empire fractured as Black Marsh, Elsweyr, and Summerset Isle seceded, while Morrowind was devastated by Red Mountain''s eruption and an Argonian invasion. Under Titus Mede II, the Empire faced the Great War (4E 171–175) with the Aldmeri Dominion, ending with the White-Gold Concordat and the loss of Hammerfell. By 4E 201, the Empire was reduced to Cyrodiil, High Rock, and Skyrim, which faced the Stormcloak and Forsworn rebellions.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('fort_neugrad', 'Fort Neugrad is a medium-sized fort located east of Falkreath and southeast of Helgen in Falkreath Hold. It is occupied by bandits', '''and'':15B ''bandits'':26B ''by'':25B ''east'':12B ''falkreath'':14B,20B ''fort'':1A,3B,10B ''helg'':18B ''hold'':21B ''in'':19B ''is'':5B,23B ''it'':22B ''locat'':11B ''medium'':8B ''medium-siz'':7B ''neugr'':2A,4B ''occupi'':24B ''of'':13B,17B ''siz'':9B ''southeast'':16B', 'falkreath', 'Fort Neugrad is a medium-sized fort located east of Falkreath.You do not know anything else about this area.', 'skyrimall, whiterun, rift reach', '', 'falkreath');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('second_treaty_of_stros_mkai', 'The Second Treaty of Stros M''Kai, signed in 4E 180, marked a significant turning point in the relationship between the Aldmeri Dominion and the Redguards of Hammerfell. This treaty concluded a decade-long conflict that arose from the Dominion''s attempts to conquer Hammerfell, which had reached a stalemate. As part of the treaty''s conditions, the Aldmeri Dominion was required to withdraw all military forces from the region, solidifying Hammerfell''s status as an independent nation, albeit a diminished one.

The treaty''s aftermath left lingering tensions between Hammerfell and the Empire. Many Redguards viewed Emperor Titus Mede II''s earlier acceptance of the White-Gold Concordat, which had concluded the Great War, as a betrayal. This agreement had required the cession of significant territory in Hammerfell to the Dominion and ultimately led to the Empire renouncing Hammerfell as a province when it protested the treaty''s terms. In response to these events, a clandestine organization known as the Remnants was formed in Hammerfell, tasked with ensuring that the Aldmeri Dominion adhered to the treaty and investigating any potential violations.', '''180'':16B ''4e'':15B ''acceptanc'':110B ''adher'':181B ''aftermath'':92B ''agreement'':127B ''albeit'':85B ''aldmeri'':27B,65B,179B ''all'':71B ''an'':82B ''and'':29B,98B,140B,185B ''any'':187B ''aros'':43B ''as'':57B,81B,123B,148B,167B ''attempts'':48B ''betrayal'':125B ''betw'':25B,96B ''cession'':131B ''clandestin'':164B ''conclud'':36B,119B ''concordat'':116B ''conditions'':63B ''conflict'':41B ''conqu'':50B ''decad'':39B ''decade-long'':38B ''diminish'':87B ''dominion'':28B,46B,66B,139B,180B ''earli'':109B ''emperor'':104B ''empir'':100B,145B ''ensuring'':176B ''events'':162B ''forc'':73B ''form'':171B ''from'':44B,74B ''gold'':115B ''great'':121B ''had'':53B,118B,128B ''hammerfell'':33B,51B,78B,97B,136B,147B,173B ''ii'':107B ''in'':14B,22B,135B,158B,172B ''independent'':83B ''investigating'':186B ''it'':152B ''kai'':12B ''known'':166B ''led'':142B ''left'':93B ''lingering'':94B ''long'':40B ''m'':11B ''many'':101B ''mark'':17B ''med'':106B ''military'':72B ''mkai'':5A ''nation'':84B ''of'':3A,9B,32B,59B,111B,132B ''one'':88B ''organization'':165B ''part'':58B ''point'':21B ''potential'':188B ''protest'':153B ''provinc'':150B ''reach'':54B ''redguards'':31B,102B ''region'':76B ''relationship'':24B ''remnants'':169B ''renouncing'':146B ''requir'':68B,129B ''respons'':159B ''s'':47B,62B,79B,91B,108B,156B ''second'':1A,7B ''sign'':13B ''significant'':19B,133B ''solidifying'':77B ''stalemat'':56B ''status'':80B ''stros'':4A,10B ''task'':174B ''tensions'':95B ''terms'':157B ''territory'':134B ''that'':42B,177B ''the'':6B,23B,26B,30B,45B,60B,64B,75B,89B,99B,112B,120B,130B,138B,144B,154B,168B,178B,183B ''thes'':161B ''this'':34B,126B ''titus'':105B ''to'':49B,69B,137B,143B,160B,182B ''treaty'':2A,8B,35B,61B,90B,155B,184B ''turning'':20B ''ultimately'':141B ''view'':103B ''violations'':189B ''war'':122B ''was'':67B,170B ''when'':151B ''which'':52B,117B ''whit'':114B ''white-gold'':113B ''with'':175B ''withdraw'':70B', 'redguard, scholar, noble', 'The Second Treaty of Stros M''Kai, signed in 4E 180, ended a decade-long conflict between the Aldmeri Dominion and the Redguards of Hammerfell. The treaty required the Dominion to withdraw its forces, confirming Hammerfell''s independence but leaving the nation weakened. The treaty followed tensions sparked by Emperor Titus Mede II''s acceptance of the White-Gold Concordat, which ceded Hammerfell territory to the Dominion. Hammerfell''s protest against these terms led to its renouncement as an Imperial province. In the aftermath, the clandestine group known as the Remnants formed to monitor Dominion compliance and address potential treaty violations.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('chim', 'CHIM, known as the "secret syllable of royalty," is an advanced state of being said to allow a person to defy all limitations. This concept, believed to be from the ancient Ehlnofex language, represents royalty, splendor, and starlight. Reaching CHIM involves a profound realization of the universe’s nature and one''s place within it, leading to an understanding of the entire scope of existence while maintaining a firm grasp on individuality. Those who achieve CHIM are rumored to possess the ability to reshape the world, with some legends claiming that Tiber Septim used this power to transform Cyrodiil from an endless jungle into a temperate land.

The process of attaining CHIM is one of the six "Walking Ways" to divinity. Vivec, a revered figure in Dunmer history, wrote extensively on CHIM, attributing its discovery to Lorkhan, who allegedly used this knowledge to enlist the Aedra in creating Mundus. Though his attempt at CHIM failed, some suggest this failure served as a cautionary example for others. CHIM is often seen as a step toward the ultimate state known as Amaranth, a union with the Godhead that resembles lucid dreaming, where two beings are required to "mantle" the Godhead, experiencing a dreamlike creation beyond all constraints.', '''ability'':83B ''achiev'':76B ''advanc'':12B ''aedr'':147B ''all'':23B,205B ''allegedly'':140B ''allow'':18B ''amaranth'':181B ''an'':11B,59B,102B ''ancient'':32B ''and'':38B,51B ''are'':78B,194B ''as'':4B,162B,172B,180B ''at'':154B ''attaining'':112B ''attempt'':153B ''attributing'':134B ''be'':29B ''being'':15B ''beings'':193B ''believ'':27B ''beyond'':204B ''cautionary'':164B ''chim'':1A,2B,41B,77B,113B,133B,155B,168B ''claiming'':91B ''concept'':26B ''constraints'':206B ''creating'':149B ''creation'':203B ''cyrodiil'':100B ''defy'':22B ''discovery'':136B ''divinity'':122B ''dreaming'':190B ''dreamlik'':202B ''dunm'':128B ''ehlnofex'':33B ''endless'':103B ''enlist'':145B ''entir'':63B ''exampl'':165B ''existenc'':66B ''experiencing'':200B ''extensively'':131B ''fail'':156B ''failur'':160B ''figur'':126B ''firm'':70B ''for'':166B ''from'':30B,101B ''godh'':186B,199B ''grasp'':71B ''his'':152B ''history'':129B ''in'':127B,148B ''individuality'':73B ''into'':105B ''involv'':42B ''is'':10B,114B,169B ''it'':56B ''its'':135B ''jungl'':104B ''knowledg'':143B ''known'':3B,179B ''land'':108B ''languag'':34B ''leading'':57B ''legends'':90B ''limitations'':24B ''lorkh'':138B ''luc'':189B ''maintaining'':68B ''mantl'':197B ''mundus'':150B ''natur'':50B ''of'':8B,14B,46B,61B,65B,111B,116B ''often'':170B ''on'':72B,132B ''one'':52B,115B ''others'':167B ''person'':20B ''plac'':54B ''possess'':81B ''pow'':97B ''process'':110B ''profound'':44B ''reaching'':40B ''realization'':45B ''represents'':35B ''requir'':195B ''resembl'':188B ''reshap'':85B ''rever'':125B ''royalty'':9B,36B ''rumor'':79B ''s'':49B,53B ''said'':16B ''scop'':64B ''secret'':6B ''seen'':171B ''septim'':94B ''serv'':161B ''six'':118B ''som'':89B,157B ''splendor'':37B ''starlight'':39B ''stat'':13B,178B ''step'':174B ''suggest'':158B ''syllabl'':7B ''temperat'':107B ''that'':92B,187B ''the'':5B,31B,47B,62B,82B,86B,109B,117B,146B,176B,185B,198B ''this'':25B,96B,142B,159B ''thos'':74B ''though'':151B ''tib'':93B ''to'':17B,21B,28B,58B,80B,84B,98B,121B,137B,144B,196B ''toward'':175B ''transform'':99B ''two'':192B ''ultimat'':177B ''understanding'':60B ''union'':183B ''univ'':48B ''used'':95B,141B ''vivec'':123B ''walking'':119B ''ways'':120B ''wher'':191B ''whil'':67B ''who'':75B,139B ''with'':88B,184B ''within'':55B ''world'':87B ''wrot'':130B', 'priest, daedra, scholar', 'CHIM is a state of being that transcends limitations, originating from the Ehlnofex language. It involves understanding the universe and one''s role in it while maintaining individuality, allowing for the power to reshape reality. Achieving CHIM is one of the "Walking Ways" to divinity and is linked to Lorkhan, who discovered it during the creation of Mundus. Tiber Septim is believed to have used CHIM to transform Cyrodiil. Vivec saw CHIM as a step toward Amaranth, a union with the Godhead that represents transcendence and a new state of creation.', 'darkelf', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('hist', 'The Hist are ancient, sentient spore trees found primarily in the swamps of Black Marsh, revered by the Argonians as both their creators and spiritual guides. Often referred to as "people of the root," Argonians share a profound bond with the Hist, symbolized through rituals involving the consumption of their sap, which grants visions and strengthens this connection. The Hist are believed to predate all mortal races, surviving the catastrophic wars of the Ehlnofey. Their collective consciousness spans across Nirn, allowing them to act as one entity, though rogue Hist, like the one in Lilmoth, occasionally sever this bond. The Hist are pivotal to Argonian life, nurturing their eggs during hatching rituals and guiding their culture through mystical communion, dreams, and visions. Marsh-born Argonians wield Hist-specific magic and are deeply tied to their roots, whereas those hatched outside Black Marsh, known as lukiul, often struggle to hear the Hist''s voice.

Legends surrounding the Hist highlight their role in major historical events, such as the Oblivion Crisis, where they rallied Argonians to invade Mehrunes Dagon''s realms, leading to a decisive victory. The Hist are also known for their strange artifacts and manifestations, such as the Dreaming Tree, which preserved the souls of an Argonian tribe during an Ayleid invasion, and the Sleeping Tree in Skyrim, speculated to be a "cousin" of the Hist from Oblivion. Hist sap, a potent and mystical substance, is capable of altering living beings, enhancing combat abilities, and even creating dangerous bloodlust when consumed improperly. Despite their enigmatic nature, the Hist remain central to Argonian identity and culture, embodying the balance between their primal origins and spiritual purpose within the greater world of Tamriel.', '''abiliti'':247B ''across'':80B ''act'':85B ''all'':66B ''allowing'':82B ''also'':191B ''altering'':242B ''an'':209B,213B ''ancient'':5B ''and'':25B,56B,114B,122B,133B,197B,216B,236B,248B,267B,276B ''are'':4B,62B,103B,134B,190B ''argoni'':106B,210B,265B ''argonians'':20B,36B,127B,176B ''artifacts'':196B ''as'':21B,31B,86B,147B,169B,200B ''ayle'':214B ''balanc'':271B ''be'':224B ''beings'':244B ''believ'':63B ''betw'':272B ''black'':15B,144B ''bloodlust'':252B ''bond'':40B,100B ''born'':126B ''both'':22B ''by'':18B ''capabl'':240B ''catastrophic'':71B ''central'':263B ''collectiv'':77B ''combat'':246B ''communion'':120B ''connection'':59B ''consciousness'':78B ''consum'':254B ''consumption'':49B ''cousin'':226B ''creating'':250B ''creators'':24B ''crisis'':172B ''cultur'':117B,268B ''dagon'':180B ''dangerous'':251B ''decisiv'':186B ''deeply'':135B ''despit'':256B ''dreaming'':202B ''dreams'':121B ''during'':111B,212B ''eggs'':110B ''ehlnofey'':75B ''embodying'':269B ''enhancing'':245B ''enigmatic'':258B ''entity'':88B ''even'':249B ''events'':167B ''for'':193B ''found'':9B ''from'':230B ''grants'':54B ''great'':281B ''guid'':27B ''guiding'':11