You do not know the alchemical effects of this ingredient. However, an alchemist might.', '', '', 'items');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('mythic_dawn', 'The Mythic Dawn, a secretive and zealous cult, worshiped Mehrunes Dagon, the Daedric Prince of Destruction. Founded centuries before its rise to infamy, the cult became widely known for its pivotal role in the Oblivion Crisis of 3E 433. Under the leadership of the enigmatic and "mad" Mankar Camoran, the cult orchestrated the assassination of Emperor Uriel Septim VII and his heirs, collapsing the divine barrier known as the Dragonfires and enabling Dagon’s forces to invade Tamriel. With sleeper agents scattered throughout the Empire and their main shrine hidden near Lake Arrius, the cult thrived in secrecy. Their ultimate defeat came through the intervention of the Hero of Kvatch, who dismantled the cult, killed Camoran and his children, and banished Mehrunes Dagon from Nirn, thus ending the Third Era.

In the centuries following their downfall, remnants of the Mythic Dawn persisted in legend and occasional resurgence. By 4E 201, the cult saw a partial revival through individuals like Silus Vesuius, who established a museum in Dawnstar to preserve their history, and Vonos, a high priest drawn to an ancient Oblivion Gate in the Velothi Mountains.', '''201'':152B ''3e'':40B ''433'':41B ''4e'':151B ''agents'':83B ''an'':181B ''ancient'':182B ''and'':8B,48B,62B,73B,88B,119B,122B,147B,174B ''arrius'':95B ''as'':70B ''assassination'':56B ''banish'':123B ''barri'':68B ''becam'':28B ''befor'':21B ''by'':150B ''cam'':104B ''camor'':51B,118B ''centuri'':20B,135B ''childr'':121B ''collapsing'':65B ''crisis'':38B ''cult'':10B,27B,53B,97B,116B,154B ''daedric'':15B ''dagon'':13B,75B,125B ''dawn'':2A,5B,143B ''dawnst'':169B ''defeat'':103B ''destruction'':18B ''dismantl'':114B ''divin'':67B ''downfall'':138B ''dragonfir'':72B ''drawn'':179B ''emperor'':58B ''empir'':87B ''enabling'':74B ''ending'':129B ''enigmatic'':47B ''establish'':165B ''following'':136B ''for'':31B ''forc'':77B ''found'':19B ''from'':126B ''gat'':184B ''heirs'':64B ''her'':110B ''hidd'':92B ''high'':177B ''his'':63B,120B ''history'':173B ''in'':35B,99B,133B,145B,168B,185B ''individuals'':160B ''infamy'':25B ''intervention'':107B ''invad'':79B ''its'':22B,32B ''kill'':117B ''known'':30B,69B ''kvatch'':112B ''lak'':94B ''leadership'':44B ''legend'':146B ''lik'':161B ''mad'':49B ''main'':90B ''mank'':50B ''mehrun'':12B,124B ''mountains'':188B ''museum'':167B ''mythic'':1A,4B,142B ''near'':93B ''nirn'':127B ''oblivion'':37B,183B ''occasional'':148B ''of'':17B,39B,45B,57B,108B,111B,140B ''orchestrat'':54B ''partial'':157B ''persist'':144B ''pivotal'':33B ''preserv'':171B ''priest'':178B ''princ'':16B ''remnants'':139B ''resurgenc'':149B ''revival'':158B ''ris'':23B ''rol'':34B ''s'':76B ''saw'':155B ''scatter'':84B ''secrecy'':100B ''secretiv'':7B ''septim'':60B ''shrin'':91B ''silus'':162B ''sleep'':82B ''tamriel'':80B ''the'':3B,14B,26B,36B,43B,46B,52B,55B,66B,71B,86B,89B,96B,101B,106B,109B,115B,130B,134B,137B,141B,153B,172B,186B ''third'':131B ''thriv'':98B ''through'':105B,159B ''throughout'':85B ''thus'':128B ''to'':24B,78B,170B,180B ''ultimat'':102B ''under'':42B ''uriel'':59B ''velothi'':187B ''vesuius'':163B ''vii'':61B ''von'':175B ''who'':113B,164B ''widely'':29B ''with'':81B ''worship'':11B ''zealous'':9B', 'daedra, scholar', '
The Mythic Dawn was a secretive cult worshiping Mehrunes Dagon, the Daedric Prince of Destruction. Known for its pivotal role in the Oblivion Crisis, the cult, led by Mankar Camoran, assassinated Emperor Uriel Septim VII and his heirs, enabling Dagon’s invasion of Tamriel. The cult operated in secrecy, with sleeper agents and a hidden shrine. The Hero of Kvatch ultimately defeated the cult, killing Camoran and banishing Dagon. In the centuries following their downfall, remnants of the Mythic Dawn lingered, with figures like Silus Vesuius and Vonos reviving the cult’s history and influence by 4E 201.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('sea_elves', 'The Maormer, also known as Sea Elves or Pyandoneans, are an elusive race of Mer native to Pyandonea, a mist-shrouded island south of the Summerset Isles. Their unique appearance, ranging from pearlescent white to blue skin, with fin-like ears and occasionally gills, reflects their adaptation to a life at sea. Under the rule of their immortal leader King Orgnum, a deathless sorcerer said to grow younger with age, the Maormer have relentlessly sought to conquer the Summerset Isles, driven by their hatred for the Altmer. Their mastery of snake magic allows them to tame sea serpents as mounts and war beasts, and their storm mages wield the elements of water, wind, and lightning to devastating effect in naval warfare.

Despite their isolation, the Maormer have clashed with Tamrielic nations throughout history, most notably in the War of the Isle in 3E 110, where their armada was defeated by the combined forces of the Psijic Order, Summerset''s navy, and the Empire. Known for their clan-based society and a culture deeply tied to the sea, the Maormer are infamous for their acrobatic sailors, serpent-like warbeasts, and vicious storm rituals. Though their ambitions have waned in recent centuries, their mysterious homeland and enduring enmity towards the Altmer ensure that the Maormer remain a haunting presence on Tamriel’s seas.', '''110'':148B ''3e'':147B ''acrobatic'':189B ''adaptation'':51B ''age'':74B ''allows'':97B ''also'':5B ''altmer'':91B,215B ''ambitions'':201B ''an'':13B ''and'':46B,105B,108B,118B,165B,175B,195B,210B ''appearanc'':33B ''are'':12B,185B ''armad'':151B ''as'':7B,103B ''at'':55B ''bas'':173B ''beasts'':107B ''blu'':39B ''by'':86B,154B ''centuri'':206B ''clan'':172B ''clan-bas'':171B ''clash'':132B ''combin'':156B ''conqu'':81B ''cultur'':177B ''deathless'':67B ''deeply'':178B ''defeat'':153B ''despit'':126B ''devastating'':121B ''driv'':85B ''ears'':45B ''effect'':122B ''elements'':114B ''elusiv'':14B ''elves'':2A,9B ''empir'':167B ''enduring'':211B ''enmity'':212B ''ensur'':216B ''fin'':43B ''fin-lik'':42B ''for'':89B,169B,187B ''forc'':157B ''from'':35B ''gills'':48B ''grow'':71B ''hatr'':88B ''haunting'':222B ''hav'':77B,131B,202B ''history'':137B ''homeland'':209B ''immortal'':62B ''in'':123B,140B,146B,204B ''infamous'':186B ''island'':25B ''isle'':145B ''isles'':30B,84B ''isolation'':128B ''king'':64B ''known'':6B,168B ''lead'':63B ''lif'':54B ''lightning'':119B ''lik'':44B,193B ''mag'':111B ''magic'':96B ''maorm'':4B,76B,130B,184B,219B ''mastery'':93B ''mer'':17B ''mist'':23B ''mist-shroud'':22B ''most'':138B ''mounts'':104B ''mysterious'':208B ''nations'':135B ''nativ'':18B ''naval'':124B ''navy'':164B ''notably'':139B ''occasionally'':47B ''of'':16B,27B,60B,94B,115B,143B,158B ''on'':224B ''or'':10B ''order'':161B ''orgnum'':65B ''pearlescent'':36B ''presenc'':223B ''psijic'':160B ''pyandone'':20B ''pyandoneans'':11B ''rac'':15B ''ranging'':34B ''recent'':205B ''reflects'':49B ''relentlessly'':78B ''remain'':220B ''rituals'':198B ''rul'':59B ''s'':163B,226B ''said'':69B ''sailors'':190B ''serpent'':192B ''serpent-lik'':191B ''serpents'':102B ''shroud'':24B ''skin'':40B ''snak'':95B ''society'':174B ''sorcer'':68B ''sought'':79B ''south'':26B ''storm'':110B,197B ''summerset'':29B,83B,162B ''tam'':100B ''tamriel'':225B ''tamrielic'':134B ''that'':217B ''the'':3B,28B,31B,50B,58B,61B,75B,82B,87B,90B,92B,109B,113B,127B,129B,141B,144B,150B,155B,159B,166B,170B,181B,183B,188B,200B,207B,214B,218B ''them'':98B ''though'':199B ''throughout'':136B ''tied'':179B ''to'':19B,38B,52B,70B,80B,99B,120B,180B ''towards'':213B ''under'':57B ''uniqu'':32B ''vicious'':196B ''wan'':203B ''war'':106B,142B ''warbeasts'':194B ''warfar'':125B ''was'':152B ''wat'':116B ''wher'':149B ''whit'':37B ''wield'':112B ''wind'':117B ''with'':41B,73B,133B ''young'':72B', 'scholar', 'The Maormer, also known as Sea Elves or Pyandoneans, are a mysterious race of Mer from the mist-shrouded island of Pyandonea. Known for their pearlescent to blue skin and sea-adapted features, they are ruled by the immortal King Orgnum, a sorcerer who grows younger with age. The Maormer harbor a deep hatred for the Altmer and seek to conquer the Summerset Isles. Mastering snake magic and storm magic, they use sea serpents and elemental forces in naval warfare. While their ambitions have waned, their history of conflict with Tamrielic nations, including the War of the Isle, and their enduring enmity with the Altmer make them a lingering threat on Tamriel''s seas.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('maormer', 'The Maormer, also known as Sea Elves or Pyandoneans, are an elusive race of Mer native to Pyandonea, a mist-shrouded island south of the Summerset Isles. Their unique appearance, ranging from pearlescent white to blue skin, with fin-like ears and occasionally gills, reflects their adaptation to a life at sea. Under the rule of their immortal leader King Orgnum, a deathless sorcerer said to grow younger with age, the Maormer have relentlessly sought to conquer the Summerset Isles, driven by their hatred for the Altmer. Their mastery of snake magic allows them to tame sea serpents as mounts and war beasts, and their storm mages wield the elements of water, wind, and lightning to devastating effect in naval warfare.

Despite their isolation, the Maormer have clashed with Tamrielic nations throughout history, most notably in the War of the Isle in 3E 110, where their armada was defeated by the combined forces of the Psijic Order, Summerset''s navy, and the Empire. Known for their clan-based society and a culture deeply tied to the sea, the Maormer are infamous for their acrobatic sailors, serpent-like warbeasts, and vicious storm rituals. Though their ambitions have waned in recent centuries, their mysterious homeland and enduring enmity towards the Altmer ensure that the Maormer remain a haunting presence on Tamriel’s seas.', '''110'':147B ''3e'':146B ''acrobatic'':188B ''adaptation'':50B ''age'':73B ''allows'':96B ''also'':4B ''altmer'':90B,214B ''ambitions'':200B ''an'':12B ''and'':45B,104B,107B,117B,164B,174B,194B,209B ''appearanc'':32B ''are'':11B,184B ''armad'':150B ''as'':6B,102B ''at'':54B ''bas'':172B ''beasts'':106B ''blu'':38B ''by'':85B,153B ''centuri'':205B ''clan'':171B ''clan-bas'':170B ''clash'':131B ''combin'':155B ''conqu'':80B ''cultur'':176B ''deathless'':66B ''deeply'':177B ''defeat'':152B ''despit'':125B ''devastating'':120B ''driv'':84B ''ears'':44B ''effect'':121B ''elements'':113B ''elusiv'':13B ''elves'':8B ''empir'':166B ''enduring'':210B ''enmity'':211B ''ensur'':215B ''fin'':42B ''fin-lik'':41B ''for'':88B,168B,186B ''forc'':156B ''from'':34B ''gills'':47B ''grow'':70B ''hatr'':87B ''haunting'':221B ''hav'':76B,130B,201B ''history'':136B ''homeland'':208B ''immortal'':61B ''in'':122B,139B,145B,203B ''infamous'':185B ''island'':24B ''isle'':144B ''isles'':29B,83B ''isolation'':127B ''king'':63B ''known'':5B,167B ''lead'':62B ''lif'':53B ''lightning'':118B ''lik'':43B,192B ''mag'':110B ''magic'':95B ''maorm'':1A,3B,75B,129B,183B,218B ''mastery'':92B ''mer'':16B ''mist'':22B ''mist-shroud'':21B ''most'':137B ''mounts'':103B ''mysterious'':207B ''nations'':134B ''nativ'':17B ''naval'':123B ''navy'':163B ''notably'':138B ''occasionally'':46B ''of'':15B,26B,59B,93B,114B,142B,157B ''on'':223B ''or'':9B ''order'':160B ''orgnum'':64B ''pearlescent'':35B ''presenc'':222B ''psijic'':159B ''pyandone'':19B ''pyandoneans'':10B ''rac'':14B ''ranging'':33B ''recent'':204B ''reflects'':48B ''relentlessly'':77B ''remain'':219B ''rituals'':197B ''rul'':58B ''s'':162B,225B ''said'':68B ''sailors'':189B ''serpent'':191B ''serpent-lik'':190B ''serpents'':101B ''shroud'':23B ''skin'':39B ''snak'':94B ''society'':173B ''sorcer'':67B ''sought'':78B ''south'':25B ''storm'':109B,196B ''summerset'':28B,82B,161B ''tam'':99B ''tamriel'':224B ''tamrielic'':133B ''that'':216B ''the'':2B,27B,30B,49B,57B,60B,74B,81B,86B,89B,91B,108B,112B,126B,128B,140B,143B,149B,154B,158B,165B,169B,180B,182B,187B,199B,206B,213B,217B ''them'':97B ''though'':198B ''throughout'':135B ''tied'':178B ''to'':18B,37B,51B,69B,79B,98B,119B,179B ''towards'':212B ''under'':56B ''uniqu'':31B ''vicious'':195B ''wan'':202B ''war'':105B,141B ''warbeasts'':193B ''warfar'':124B ''was'':151B ''wat'':115B ''wher'':148B ''whit'':36B ''wield'':111B ''wind'':116B ''with'':40B,72B,132B ''young'':71B', 'scholar', '
The Maormer, or Sea Elves, are an elusive race of Mer native to the mist-covered island of Pyandonea, south of the Summerset Isles. With pearlescent white to blue skin, fin-like ears, and sometimes gills, they are adapted to life at sea. Led by the immortal King Orgnum, a sorcerer who grows younger with age, the Maormer seek to conquer the Summerset Isles, driven by their hatred for the Altmer. Masters of snake magic and storm magic, they use sea serpents and elemental powers in naval warfare. Though their ambitions have lessened, their enmity toward the Altmer and mysterious homeland make them a persistent threat on the seas of Tamriel.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('falmer', 'The Falmer, also called Snow Ghosts or Betrayed, are blind, degenerated descendants of the ancient Snow Elves who now dwell in Skyrim''s dark depths. Once a proud and prosperous race of Mer, the Snow Elves were driven underground after centuries of war with the invading Nords. Seeking refuge with the Dwemer, they were forced into servitude, fed toxic fungi that blinded them, and subjected to generations of cruelty that twisted them into the creatures now feared as Falmer. Pale, hunched, and goblin-like, they have become a symbol of tragic corruption, stalking intruders in the underground labyrinths of Dwemer ruins and beyond. Nordic folklore even attributes their disfigurement to Ysgramor cutting their noses off to distinguish them from other Elves.

Despite their degeneration, Falmer have adapted to subterranean life with remarkable resilience. They cultivate fungi, fish the underground waterways, and breed the fearsome Chaurus, whose chitin they use to craft weapons, armor, and even dwellings. Their society is tribal and brutal, relying on raiding surface dwellers for slaves and resources, and they harbor an enduring hatred for all but their own. While they are often dismissed as monsters, some—including Knight-Paladin Gelebor, the last known uncorrupted Snow Elf—view them as tragic figures. Gelebor holds hope that they may one day reclaim their former identity, though the Falmer''s increasing aggression suggests their future may be one of conquest rather than redemption.', '''adapt'':129B ''after'':41B ''aggression'':226B ''all'':181B ''also'':4B ''an'':177B ''ancient'':16B ''and'':30B,65B,83B,104B,143B,156B,163B,172B,174B ''are'':10B,187B ''armor'':155B ''as'':79B,190B,206B ''attribut'':109B ''be'':231B ''becom'':89B ''betray'':9B ''beyond'':105B ''blind'':11B,63B ''bre'':144B ''brutal'':164B ''but'':182B ''call'':5B ''centuri'':42B ''chaurus'':147B ''chitin'':149B ''conquest'':234B ''corruption'':94B ''craft'':153B ''creatur'':76B ''cruelty'':70B ''cultivat'':137B ''cutting'':114B ''dark'':25B ''day'':216B ''degenerat'':12B ''degeneration'':126B ''depths'':26B ''descendants'':13B ''despit'':124B ''disfigurement'':111B ''dismiss'':189B ''distinguish'':119B ''driv'':39B ''dwell'':21B ''dwellers'':169B ''dwellings'':158B ''dwem'':53B,102B ''elf'':203B ''elves'':18B,37B,123B ''enduring'':178B ''even'':108B,157B ''falm'':1A,3B,80B,127B,223B ''fear'':78B ''fearsom'':146B ''fed'':59B ''figur'':208B ''fish'':139B ''folklor'':107B ''for'':170B,180B ''forc'':56B ''form'':219B ''from'':121B ''fungi'':61B,138B ''futur'':229B ''gelebor'':197B,209B ''generations'':68B ''ghosts'':7B ''goblin'':85B ''goblin-lik'':84B ''harbor'':176B ''hatr'':179B ''hav'':88B,128B ''holds'':210B ''hop'':211B ''hunch'':82B ''identity'':220B 