INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('an_accounting_of_the_scrolls', 'An Accounting of the Scrolls, written by Quintus Nerevelus, Former Imperial Librarian, is a reflective exploration of the mysteries surrounding the Elder Scrolls and the author''s personal journey into the Cult of the Ancestor Moth. The book begins with Nerevelus''s attempt to catalog the Scrolls to prevent theft or loss, only to confront their unquantifiable nature. His efforts reveal contradictions and anomalies, culminating in a moment where the number of Scrolls in a repository seemingly changes before his eyes. This experience leads him to abandon his skepticism and join the Cult to seek deeper understanding.

Thematically, the book delves into the limits of human comprehension, the allure of forbidden knowledge, and the transition from intellectual curiosity to spiritual surrender. This standalone work portrays the Elder Scrolls as enigmatic artifacts that defy conventional logic, underscoring their connection to fate, prophecy, and metaphysical truths beyond mortal grasp.', '''abandon'':92B ''accounting'':2A,7B ''allur'':114B ''an'':1A,6B ''ancestor'':40B ''and'':29B,68B,95B,118B,147B ''anomali'':69B ''artifacts'':136B ''as'':134B ''attempt'':48B ''author'':31B ''befor'':84B ''begins'':44B ''beyond'':150B ''book'':43B,105B ''by'':12B ''catalog'':50B ''chang'':83B ''comprehension'':112B ''confront'':60B ''connection'':143B ''contradictions'':67B ''conventional'':139B ''culminating'':70B ''cult'':37B,98B ''curiosity'':123B ''deep'':101B ''defy'':138B ''delv'':106B ''efforts'':65B ''elder'':27B,132B ''enigmatic'':135B ''experienc'':88B ''exploration'':21B ''eyes'':86B ''fat'':145B ''forbidd'':116B ''form'':15B ''from'':121B ''grasp'':152B ''him'':90B ''his'':64B,85B,93B ''hum'':111B ''imperial'':16B ''in'':71B,79B ''intellectual'':122B ''into'':35B,107B ''is'':18B ''join'':96B ''journey'':34B ''knowledg'':117B ''leads'':89B ''librari'':17B ''limits'':109B ''logic'':140B ''loss'':57B ''metaphysical'':148B ''moment'':73B ''mortal'':151B ''moth'':41B ''mysteri'':24B ''natur'':63B ''nerevelus'':14B,46B ''numb'':76B ''of'':3A,8B,22B,38B,77B,110B,115B ''only'':58B ''or'':56B ''personal'':33B ''portrays'':130B ''prevent'':54B ''prophecy'':146B ''quintus'':13B ''reflectiv'':20B ''repository'':81B ''reveal'':66B ''s'':32B,47B ''scrolls'':5A,10B,28B,52B,78B,133B ''seek'':100B ''seemingly'':82B ''skepticism'':94B ''spiritual'':125B ''standalon'':128B ''surrend'':126B ''surrounding'':25B ''that'':137B ''the'':4A,9B,23B,26B,30B,36B,39B,42B,51B,61B,75B,97B,104B,108B,113B,119B,131B,142B ''theft'':55B ''thematically'':103B ''this'':87B,127B ''to'':49B,53B,59B,91B,99B,124B,144B ''transition'':120B ''truths'':149B ''underscoring'':141B ''understanding'':102B ''unquantifi'':62B ''wher'':74B ''with'':45B ''work'':129B ''writt'':11B', 'scholar', 'An Accounting of the Scrolls, written by Quintus Nerevelus, Former Imperial Librarian, explores the mysteries of the Elder Scrolls through the author''s attempts to catalog them, only to encounter their unquantifiable and paradoxical nature. The book reflects on the limits of human understanding, the allure of forbidden knowledge, and the shift from intellectual curiosity to spiritual surrender, portraying the Scrolls as enigmatic artifacts tied to fate and metaphysical truths beyond mortal comprehension.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('the_adabal-a', '
The Adabal-a, written by Morihaus, is an ancient and revered text traditionally believed to be the memoirs of Morihaus, the winged-bull demigod and consort of Alessia, the Slave Queen. It recounts pivotal moments in early Cyrodiilic history, including Pelinal Whitestrake''s final words before his death, Alessia''s suffering and defiance during the Ayleid enslavement, and the origins of her many titles. The work paints a vivid picture of the brutal oppression under Ayleid rule and the resilience of Alessia as she rose to lead her people to freedom. Themes of divine intervention, mortal struggle, and the cost of liberation permeate the text, highlighting the blend of myth and history that defines Cyrodiilic lore.

This singular work also explores the mystical bond between Morihaus and Alessia, offering insight into her numerous names and their significance. Her transformation from a slave to the first Empress of Cyrodiil is framed as a divine journey, with Morihaus providing personal reflections on her courage and legacy. The Adabal-a serves as both a historical document and a spiritual narrative, emphasizing themes of sacrifice, justice, and the enduring power of human resolve in the face of tyranny.', '''adabal'':3A,6B,172B ''adabal-'':2A,5B,171B ''alessi'':33B,54B,87B,133B ''also'':125B ''an'':12B ''ancient'':13B ''and'':14B,30B,57B,63B,83B,103B,116B,132B,140B,168B,180B,189B ''as'':88B,156B,175B ''ayle'':61B,81B ''be'':20B ''befor'':51B ''believ'':18B ''betw'':130B ''blend'':113B ''bond'':129B ''both'':176B ''brutal'':78B ''bull'':28B ''by'':9B ''consort'':31B ''cost'':105B ''courag'':167B ''cyrodiil'':153B ''cyrodiilic'':43B,120B ''death'':53B ''defianc'':58B ''defin'':119B ''demigod'':29B ''divin'':99B,158B ''document'':179B ''during'':59B ''early'':42B ''emphasizing'':184B ''empress'':151B ''enduring'':191B ''enslavement'':62B ''explor'':126B ''fac'':198B ''final'':49B ''first'':150B ''fram'':155B ''freedom'':96B ''from'':145B ''her'':67B,93B,137B,143B,166B ''highlighting'':111B ''his'':52B ''historical'':178B ''history'':44B,117B ''hum'':194B ''in'':41B,196B ''including'':45B ''insight'':135B ''intervention'':100B ''into'':136B ''is'':11B,154B ''it'':37B ''journey'':159B ''justic'':188B ''lead'':92B ''legacy'':169B ''liberation'':107B ''lor'':121B ''many'':68B ''memoirs'':22B ''moments'':40B ''morihaus'':10B,24B,131B,161B ''mortal'':101B ''mystical'':128B ''myth'':115B ''nam'':139B ''narrativ'':183B ''numerous'':138B ''of'':23B,32B,66B,76B,86B,98B,106B,114B,152B,186B,193B,199B ''offering'':134B ''on'':165B ''oppression'':79B ''origins'':65B ''paints'':72B ''pelinal'':46B ''peopl'':94B ''permeat'':108B ''personal'':163B ''pictur'':75B ''pivotal'':39B ''pow'':192B ''prov