Ancient Tales of the Dwemer, Part XI: Azura and the Box, you do not know the author of the book and have limited knowledge of it, explores the Dwemer’s skepticism toward divine power through the story of a sage who tests Azura’s omniscience. Thematically, the book delves into the pursuit of knowledge, mortal skepticism, and the complex relationship between gods and mortals, with a focus on the Dwemer’s irreverence and their mysterious disappearance.', '', '', 'lore');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('the_bear_of_markarth', 'The Bear of Markarth, written by Arrianus Arius, Imperial Scholar, offers a critical examination of Ulfric Stormcloak''s controversial conquest of the Reach during the Forsworn Uprising. From 4E 174 to 176, the Forsworn established a peaceful, independent kingdom while the Empire was preoccupied with the Great War. However, Ulfric’s militia stormed Markarth, claiming the city for Skyrim. The book condemns Ulfric’s actions during and after the siege, detailing his ruthless execution of Forsworn officials, brutal torture of civilians, and indiscriminate killings of Nords and Reachfolk who did not support him. This bloodshed forced the Empire to accept Ulfric''s demands for free Talos worship, despite violating the White-Gold Concordat.

Themes of the book include the complexities of war, the morality of rebellion, and the cost of political compromise. It portrays Ulfric as a polarizing figure, revered as a hero by some and reviled as a war criminal by others. This single-volume account highlights the tension between Nord nationalism and the broader obligations of the Empire, raising questions about the price of leadership and loyalty.', '''174'':34B ''176'':36B ''4e'':33B ''about'':179B ''accept'':104B ''account'':163B ''actions'':69B ''after'':72B ''and'':71B,86B,91B,132B,151B,170B,184B ''arius'':12B ''arrianus'':11B ''as'':141B,146B,153B ''bear'':2A,6B ''betw'':167B ''bloodsh'':99B ''book'':65B,122B ''broad'':172B ''brutal'':82B ''by'':10B,149B,157B ''city'':61B ''civilians'':85B ''claiming'':59B ''complexiti'':125B ''compromis'':137B ''concordat'':118B ''condemns'':66B ''conquest'':24B ''controversial'':23B ''cost'':134B ''criminal'':156B ''critical'':17B ''demands'':107B ''despit'':112B ''detailing'':75B ''did'':94B ''during'':28B,70B ''empir'':46B,102B,176B ''establish'':39B ''examination'':18B ''execution'':78B ''figur'':144B ''for'':62B,108B ''forc'':100B ''forsworn'':30B,38B,80B ''fre'':109B ''from'':32B ''gold'':117B ''great'':51B ''her'':148B ''highlights'':164B ''him'':97B ''his'':76B ''howev'':53B ''imperial'':13B ''includ'':123B ''independent'':42B ''indiscriminat'':87B ''it'':138B ''killings'':88B ''kingdom'':43B ''leadership'':183B ''loyalty'':185B ''markarth'':4A,8B,58B ''militi'':56B ''morality'':129B ''nationalism'':169B ''nord'':168B ''nords'':90B ''not'':95B ''obligations'':173B ''of'':3A,7B,19B,25B,79B,84B,89B,120B,126B,130B,135B,174B,182B ''offers'':15B ''officials'':81B ''others'':158B ''peaceful'':41B ''polarizing'':143B ''political'':136B ''portrays'':139B ''preoccupi'':48B ''pric'':181B ''questions'':178B ''raising'':177B ''reach'':27B ''reachfolk'':92B ''rebellion'':131B ''rever'':145B ''revil'':152B ''ruthless'':77B ''s'':22B,55B,68B,106B ''schol'':14B ''sieg'':74B ''sin