Julianos, the God of Wisdom and Logic, is a Divine revered for his association with knowledge, law, history, and intellectual pursuits. Known as a patron of scholars, wizards, and legal minds, he emphasizes learning, logic, and the pursuit of truth. His symbol, a triangle, represents the structure of his teachings, while his temples, such as the Schools of Julianos, serve as centers of education and preservation of knowledge, including the Elder Scrolls. Originally worshipped by the Nords as Jhunal, he became part of the Divines as Julianos, a protector of justice and wisdom. Artifacts like the Shield of the Crusader further symbolize his role, and his celestial presence as JHUNAL marks the eye of the Mage constellation in Mundus.', '', '', 'figures');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('lorkhan', 'Lorkhan, also known as the Missing God, is a central figure in Tamrielic myth, often regarded as the Creator, Trickster, and Tester of mortals. Revered and reviled, Lorkhan''s influence is woven into every culture’s mythology, and he is credited with initiating the creation of the mortal plane, Nirn. He convinced the et''Ada, the Original Spirits, to create Mundus, the world, disrupting the cosmic order. Following the world''s creation, Lorkhan was separated from his divine essence—most commonly through the removal of his Heart—and exiled from the divine realm. This event, referred to as the "shattering of Lorkhan," is depicted as a punishment for his role in the creation of the flawed mortal realm, though some legends suggest he willingly accepted this fate.

The perceptions of Lorkhan vary greatly between different races. To the Elves, he is a deceiver responsible for trapping them in the mortal realm, severing their connection to the spiritual plane. The Altmer view him as the ultimate antagonist, a being who imprisoned them in a world of suffering and limitation. Conversely, humans, especially Nords and Imperials, honor him as a heroic figure. In Skyrim, he is worshipped as Shor, the god who sacrificed himself to create the world. Redguards know him as Sep, a trickster who helped shape the mortal world but was cursed for his transgressions. Reachmen revere him as the Spirit of Man, while Khajiit associate him with Lorkhaj, the Moon Beast, whose heart''s corruption birthed the dark dro-m’Athra. Despite his many names and forms, Lorkhan remains a symbol of the mortal struggle, embodying both the potential and the suffering of the world he helped create.', '''accept'':126B ''ada'':56B ''also'':3B ''altmer'':161B ''and'':22B,27B,39B,89B,178B,184B,259B,273B ''antagonist'':167B ''as'':5B,18B,99B,106B,164B,188B,197B,211B,230B ''associat'':237B ''athra'':254B ''beast'':243B ''being'':169B ''betw'':135B ''birth'':248B ''both'':270B ''but'':221B ''central'':11B ''commonly'':82B ''connection'':155B ''conversely'':180B ''convinc'':53B ''corruption'':247B ''cosmic'':67B ''creat'':61B,205B,281B ''creation'':46B,73B,114B ''creator'':20B ''credit'':42B ''cultur'':36B ''curs'':223B ''dark'':250B ''deceiv'':144B ''depict'':105B ''despit'':255B ''different'':136B ''disrupting'':65B ''divin'':79B,93B ''dro'':252B ''dro-m'':251B ''elves'':140B ''embodying'':269B ''especially'':182B ''essenc'':80B ''et'':55B ''event'':96B ''every'':35B ''exil'':90B ''fat'':128B ''figur'':12B,191B ''flaw'':117B ''following'':69B ''for'':109B,146B,224B ''forms'':260B ''from'':77B,91B ''god'':8B,200B ''greatly'':134B ''heart'':88B,245B ''help'':216B,280B ''heroic'':190B ''him'':163B,187B,210B,229B,238B ''himself'':203B ''his'':78B,87B,110B,225B,256B ''honor'':186B ''humans'':181B ''imperials'':185B ''imprison'':171B ''in'':13B,112B,149B,173B,192B ''influenc'':31B ''initiating'':44B ''into'':34B ''is'':9B,32B,41B,104B,142B,195B ''khajiit'':236B ''know'':209B ''known'':4B ''legends'':122B ''limitation'':179B ''lorkh'':1A,2B,29B,74B,103B,132B,261B ''lorkhaj'':240B ''m'':253B ''man'':234B ''many'':257B ''missing'':7B ''moon'':242B ''mortal'':49B,118B,151B,219B,267B ''mortals'':25B ''most'':81B ''mundus'':62B ''myth'':15B ''mythology'':38B ''nam'':258B ''nirn'':51B ''nords'':183B ''of'':24B,47B,86B,102B,115B,131B,176B,233B,265B,276B ''often'':16B ''order'':68B ''original'':58B ''perceptions'':130B ''plan'':50B,159B ''potential'':272B ''punishment'':108B ''rac'':137B ''reachm'':227B ''realm'':94B,119B,152B ''redguards'':208B ''referr'':97B ''regard'':17B ''remains'':262B ''removal'':85B ''respons'':145B ''rever'':26B,228B ''revil'':28B ''rol'':111B ''s'':30B,37B,72B,246B ''sacrific'':202B ''sep'':212B ''separat'':76B ''severing'':153B ''shap'':217B ''shattering'':101B ''shor'':198B ''skyrim'':193B ''som'':121B ''spirit'':232B ''spirits'':59B ''spiritual'':158B ''struggl'':268B ''suffering'':177B,275B ''suggest'':123B ''symbol'':264B ''tamrielic'':14B ''test'':23B ''the'':6B,19B,45B,48B,54B,57B,63B,66B,70B,84B,92B,100B,113B,116B,129B,139B,150B,154B,157B,160B,165B,199B,206B,218B,231B,241B,249B,266B,271B,274B,277B ''them'':148B,172B ''this'':95B,127B ''though'':120B ''through'':83B ''to'':60B,98B,138B,156B,204B ''transgressions'':226B ''trapping'':147B ''trickst'':21B,214B ''ultimat'':166B ''vary'':133B ''view'':162B ''was'':75B,222B ''whil'':235B ''who'':170B,201B,215B ''whos'':244B ''willingly'':125B ''with'':43B,239B ''world'':64B,71B,175B,207B,220B,278B ''worshipp'':196B ''wov'':33B', 'priest, scholar', 'Lorkhan, the Missing God, is a complex figure in Tamrielic myth, known as the Creator, Trickster, and Tester of mortals. He is credited with initiating the creation of Nirn by convincing the et''Ada to shape the mortal realm, disrupting the cosmic order. His divine essence was removed after Nirn''s creation, an act seen as either punishment or a willing sacrifice. Perceptions of Lorkhan vary widely: Elves view him as a deceiver who trapped them in mortality, while humans, such as Nords and Imperials, honor him as a heroic figure, with the Nords worshipping him as Shor. Redguards, Khajiit, and Reachmen each interpret him through their own cultural lenses, associating him with sacrifice, trickery, and cosmic struggle. Lorkhan symbolizes both the hardships and the potential of the mortal world he helped create.', '', '', 'figures');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('laila', 'Laila Law-Giver is the Jarl of Riften, residing in Mistveil Keep. While she holds the title of Jarl, the extent of her actual authority over the citizens of the Rift is often called into question.', '''actual'':26B ''authority'':27B ''call'':36B ''citizens'':30B ''extent'':23B ''giv'':5B ''her'':25B ''holds'':17B ''in'':12B ''into'':37B ''is'':6B,34B ''jarl'':8B,21B ''keep'':14B ''lail'':1A,2B ''law'':4B ''law-giv'':3B ''mistveil'':13B ''of'':9B,20B,24B,31B ''often'':35B ''over'':28B ''question'':38B ''residing'':11B ''rift'':10B,33B ''she'':16B ''the'':7B,18B,22B,29B,32B ''titl'':19B ''whil'':15B', 'scholar, noble', 'Laila Law-Giver is the Jarl of Riften, residing in Mistveil Keep. Although she holds the title of Jarl, her actual authority over the citizens of the Rift is often questioned.', '', '', 'figures');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('elisif', 'Elisif the Fair is the Jarl of Solitude and the widow of High King Torygg. With aspirations of becoming the High Queen of Skyrim, she is often regarded as a potential leader, although she feels the timing is not yet right to formally express her ambitions. Elisif harbors doubts about entrusting the ongoing war effort to General Tullius, feeling he may not fully represent the needs of Skyrim, yet she sees no viable alternatives.', '''about'':51B ''alternativ'':75B ''although'':34B ''ambitions'':47B ''and'':10B ''as'':30B ''aspirations'':18B ''becoming'':20B ''doubts'':50B ''effort'':56B ''elisif'':1A,2B,48B ''entrusting'':52B ''express'':45B ''fair'':4B ''feeling'':60B ''feels'':36B ''formally'':44B ''fully'':64B ''general'':58B ''harbors'':49B ''her'':46B ''high'':14B,22B ''is'':5B,27B,39B ''jarl'':7B ''king'':15B ''lead'':33B ''may'':62B ''needs'':67B ''not'':40B,63B ''of'':8B,13B,19B,24B,68B ''often'':28B ''ongoing'':54B ''potential'':32B ''que'':23B ''regard'':29B ''represent'':65B ''right'':42B ''sees'':72B ''she'':26B,35B,71B ''skyrim'':25B,69B ''solitud'':9B ''the'':3B,6B,11B,21B,37B,53B,66B ''timing'':38B ''to'':43B,57B ''torygg'':16B ''tullius'':59B ''viabl'':74B ''war'':55B ''widow'':12B ''with'':17B ''yet'':41B,70B', 'scholar, noble', 'Elisif the Fair is the Jarl of Solitude and the widow of High King Torygg. She aspires to become High Queen of Skyrim but believes the time is not yet right to express her ambitions. Elisif has doubts about General Tullius''s leadership in the war, feeling he may not fully represent Skyrim''s needs, though she sees no better alternatives.', '', '', 'figures');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('chicken_egg', 'Chicken''s Eggs can be harvested from chickens'' nests, commonly found at most farms. Alchemy effects: Resist Magic, Damage Magicka Regen, Waterbreathing, and Lingering Damage Stamina.', '''alchemy'':17B ''and'':25B ''at'':14B ''be'':7B ''can'':6B ''chick'':1A,3B ''chickens'':10B ''commonly'':12B ''damag'':21B,27B ''effects'':18B ''egg'':2A ''eggs'':5B ''farms'':16B ''found'':13B ''from'':9B ''harvest'':8B ''lingering'':26B ''magic'':20B ''magick'':22B ''most'':15B ''nests'':11B ''reg'':23B ''resist'':19B ''s'':4B ''stamin'':28B ''waterbreathing'':24B', 'alchemist', 'Chicken''s Eggs can be harvested from chickens'' nests, commonly found at most farms 
You do not know the alchemical effects of this ingredient. However, an alchemist might.', '', '', 'items');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('trinimac', 'Trinimac, known as the Warrior and the Paragon, was a prominent god of the early Aldmer and considered one of the most powerful et''Ada. Revered as the champion of Auri-El and a warrior spirit, Trinimac played a significant role in Aldmeri legends, particularly in the conflict against Lorkhan. He was known for his strength and leadership, often depicted holding his sword, Penitent (Vosh Rakh, the "Blade of Courage"). Trinimac was especially venerated by the Altmer, Ayleids, and early Orcs, and was regarded as a god who stood for strength, honor, and unity. However, his legacy is deeply entwined with his transformation into Malacath, a Daedric Prince, after an encounter with Boethiah.

According to legend, Boethiah consumed Trinimac and took on his form to spread new teachings among the Aldmer, leading to the creation of the Chimer and Orcs. Boethiah''s act of devouring Trinimac resulted in his followers transforming into the Orsimer (Orcs), and Trinimac himself was reborn as Malacath, a weaker and vengeful version of his former self. While many Orcs continued to worship Malacath, others maintained belief in the purity of Trinimac. During the Second Era, leaders like King Kurog and King Gortwog revived the worship of Trinimac, positioning him as a god of unity and civilization, in contrast to the savage nature associated with Malacath. Trinimac’s story remains central to the identity and history of the Orcs and is a key figure in the cultural divergence between Chimer and Orsimer.', '''according'':116B ''act'':145B ''ada'':26B ''after'':111B ''against'':51B ''aldmer'':17B,133B ''aldmeri'':45B ''altmer'':79B ''among'':131B ''an'':112B ''and'':7B,18B,35B,59B,81B,84B,95B,122B,141B,158B,167B,197B,212B,231B,236B,247B ''as'':4B,28B,87B,163B,207B ''associat'':220B ''auri'':33B ''auri-el'':32B ''ayleids'':80B ''belief'':183B ''betw'':245B ''blad'':70B ''boethiah'':115B,119B,143B ''by'':77B ''central'':227B ''champion'':30B ''chim'':140B,246B ''civilization'':213B ''conflict'':50B ''consider'':19B ''consum'':120B ''continu'':177B ''contrast'':215B ''courag'':72B ''creation'':137B ''cultural'':243B ''daedric'':109B ''deeply'':101B ''depict'':62B ''devouring'':147B ''divergenc'':244B ''during'':189B ''early'':16B,82B ''encount'':113B ''entwin'':102B ''especially'':75B ''et'':25B ''figur'':240B ''followers'':152B ''for'':56B,92B ''form'':126B,172B ''god'':13B,89B,209B ''gortwog'':199B ''him'':206B ''himself'':160B ''his'':57B,64B,98B,104B,125B,151B,171B ''history'':232B ''holding'':63B ''honor'':94B ''howev'':97B ''identity'':230B ''in'':44B,48B,150B,184B,214B,241B ''into'':106B,154B ''is'':100B,237B ''key'':239B ''king'':195B,198B ''known'':3B,55B ''kurog'':