Sentinel, the capital of the Kingdom of Sentinel in Hammerfell, is a vital cultural and political center overlooking the Iliac Bay. Founded by the Redguard warrior-sailors during the Ra Gada migration, the city is known for its architecture, including the grand Sentinel Palace, and its thriving market streets. Despite its desert surroundings, Sentinel has remained an important trade hub throughout history, facing challenges such as pirate raids, necromantic threats, and internal conflict between the Crown and Forebear factions. Today, Sentinel stands as a symbol of Redguard resilience, uniting its people and blending cultural heritage with a history of overcoming adversity.', '', '', 'locationother');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('wayrest', 'Wayrest, a prominent city in the Stormhaven region of High Rock, serves as the capital of the kingdom of the same name. Located at the mouth of the Bjoulsae River on the Iliac Bay, it is a major hub of commerce, culture, and politics in Tamriel. Divided into seven districts, including the bustling Market Square, the Temple District, and the docks along Iliac Bay, Wayrest boasts notable landmarks such as Castle Wayrest, the Temple of Akatosh, and the Wayrest Sewers. Its strategic position has fostered significant trade, making it one of the wealthiest cities in High Rock. Wayrest is also a cultural influence, shaping traditions such as the New Life Festival through its Breton-inspired aesthetics.

Founded as a fishing village in 1E 800, Wayrest rose to prominence after the Fall of Orsinium and became a kingdom by 1E 1100. The city has withstood numerous sieges, such as those by Reachmen under Durcorach the Black Drake and King Ranser during the Ranser''s War. Throughout the eras, Wayrest''s history has been shaped by political intrigue, including the Daggerfall Covenant''s formation, royal disputes, and the Warp in the West. In the Fourth Era, it faced threats such as a corsair siege in 4E 188, where the Forgotten Hero played a pivotal role in its defense. Today, Wayrest remains a testament to Breton resilience and prosperity, thriving as a cultural and economic beacon in Tamriel.', '''1100'':142B ''188'':207B ''1e'':125B,141B ''4e'':206B ''800'':126B ''aesthetics'':118B ''after'':131B ''akatosh'':77B ''along'':63B ''also'':101B ''and'':44B,60B,78B,136B,159B,187B,227B,233B ''as'':14B,71B,108B,120B,150B,201B,230B ''at'':25B ''bay'':35B,65B ''beacon'':235B ''becam'':137B ''been'':174B ''bjoulsa'':30B ''black'':157B ''boasts'':67B ''breton'':116B,225B ''breton-inspir'':115B ''bustling'':54B ''by'':140B,152B,176B ''capital'':16B ''castl'':72B ''citi'':95B ''city'':5B,144B ''commerc'':42B ''cors'':203B ''covenant'':182B ''cultur'':43B ''cultural'':103B,232B ''daggerfall'':181B ''defens'':218B ''disput'':186B ''district'':59B ''districts'':51B ''divid'':48B ''docks'':62B ''drak'':158B ''durcorach'':155B ''during'':162B ''economic'':234B ''fac'':198B ''fall'':133B ''festival'':112B ''fishing'':122B ''forgott'':210B ''formation'':184B ''foster'':86B ''found'':119B ''fourth'':195B ''her'':211B ''high'':11B,97B ''history'':172B ''hub'':40B ''iliac'':34B,64B ''in'':6B,46B,96B,124B,190B,193B,205B,216B,236B ''including'':52B,179B ''influenc'':104B ''inspir'':117B ''into'':49B ''intrig'':178B ''is'':37B,100B ''it'':36B,90B,197B ''its'':82B,114B,217B ''king'':160B ''kingdom'':19B,139B ''landmarks'':69B ''lif'':111B ''locat'':24B ''major'':39B ''making'':89B ''market'':55B ''mouth'':27B ''nam'':23B ''new'':110B ''notabl'':68B ''numerous'':147B ''of'':10B,17B,20B,28B,41B,76B,92B,134B ''on'':32B ''one'':91B ''orsinium'':135B ''pivotal'':214B ''play'':212B ''political'':177B ''politics'':45B ''position'':84B ''prominenc'':130B ''prominent'':4B ''prosperity'':228B ''rans'':161B,164B ''reachm'':153B ''region'':9B ''remains'':221B ''resilienc'':226B ''riv'':31B ''rock'':12B,98B ''rol'':215B ''ros'':128B ''royal'':185B ''s'':165B,171B,183B ''sam'':22B ''serv'':13B ''sev'':50B ''sewers'':81B ''shap'':175B ''shaping'':105B ''sieg'':148B,204B ''significant'':87B ''squar'':56B ''stormhav'':8B ''strategic'':83B ''such'':70B,107B,149B,200B ''tamriel'':47B,237B ''templ'':58B,75B ''testament'':223B ''the'':7B,15B,18B,21B,26B,29B,33B,53B,57B,61B,74B,79B,93B,109B,132B,143B,156B,163B,168B,180B,188B,191B,194B,209B ''thos'':151B ''threats'':199B ''thriving'':229B ''through'':113B ''throughout'':167B ''to'':129B,224B ''today'':219B ''trad'':88B ''traditions'':106B ''under'':154B ''villag'':123B ''war'':166B ''warp'':189B ''wayrest'':1A,2B,66B,73B,80B,99B,127B,170B,220B ''wealthiest'':94B ''west'':192B ''wher'':208B ''withstood'':146B', 'scholar, breton', '
Wayrest, the capital of the Kingdom of Wayrest in High Rock, is a thriving city located at the mouth of the Bjoulsae River on Iliac Bay. Known for its strategic position, Wayrest is a major hub of commerce and culture, featuring landmarks like Castle Wayrest and the Temple of Akatosh. Founded as a fishing village in 1E 800, it became a kingdom by 1E 1100 and has since withstood numerous sieges, including those during the Ranser''s War. With a rich history of political intrigue and trade, Wayrest continues to prosper in the Fourth Era, symbolizing Breton resilience and economic strength in Tamriel.', '', '', 'locationother');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('balmora', '
Balmora, meaning "Stonewood" or "Stoneforest" in the Dark Elvish tongue, was a prominent city in Vvardenfell, serving as the seat of House Hlaalu''s power and the second-largest settlement after Vivec City. Located in the river valley of the West Gash, on the banks of the Odai River, the city was divided into three key districts: the bustling Commercial District, the residential Labor Town, and the affluent High Town. Known for its strategic trade location and Hlaalu-style architecture, Balmora played a vital role in the economic and political landscape of Vvardenfell, acting as a gateway between the region''s rugged landscapes and fertile coastlines. Significant landmarks included the Hlaalu Council Manor, the Tribunal Temple, and the Moonmoth Imperial Legion Fort.

Historically, Balmora was initially a House Redoran stronghold before it was transformed by House Hlaalu through strategic construction projects and business maneuvers. During the Third Era, it grew under Imperial influence, becoming a hub of trade and administration. It played a central role in the Nerevarine Prophecy when the outlander destined to fulfill the prophecy operated from Balmora under the guidance of Caius Cosades. However, the city was devastated in the Red Year (4E 5) due to the eruption of Red Mountain, leaving it in ruins. Rebuilt over time, Balmora persisted into the late Fourth Era as a symbol of resilience, though its exact state in later years remains ambiguous, with conflicting accounts suggesting its abandonment or continued habitation.', '''4e'':199B ''5'':200B ''abandonment'':241B ''accounts'':238B ''acting'':97B ''administration'':163B ''affluent'':70B ''after'':33B ''ambiguous'':235B ''and'':27B,68B,79B,92B,107B,120B,145B,162B ''architectur'':83B ''as'':19B,98B,222B ''balmor'':1A,2B,84B,127B,183B,215B ''banks'':47B ''becoming'':157B ''befor'':134B ''betw'':101B ''business'':146B ''bustling'':61B ''by'':138B ''caius'':188B ''central'':167B ''city'':15B,35B,53B,192B ''coastlin'':109B ''commercial'':62B ''conflicting'':237B ''construction'':143B ''continu'':243B ''cosad'':189B ''council'':115B ''dark'':9B ''destin'':176B ''devastat'':194B ''district'':63B ''districts'':59B ''divid'':55B ''due'':201B ''during'':148B ''economic'':91B ''elvish'':10B ''eruption'':204B ''exact'':229B ''fertil'':108B ''for'':74B ''fort'':125B ''fourth'':220B ''from'':182B ''fulfill'':178B ''gash'':44B ''gateway'':100B ''grew'':153B ''guidanc'':186B ''habitation'':244B ''high'':71B ''historically'':126B ''hlaalu'':24B,81B,114B,140B ''hlaalu-styl'':80B ''hous'':23B,131B,139B ''howev'':190B ''hub'':159B ''imperial'':123B,155B ''in'':7B,16B,37B,89B,169B,195B,210B,231B ''includ'':112B ''influenc'':156B ''initially'':129B ''into'':56B,217B ''it'':135B,152B,164B,209B ''its'':75B,228B,240B ''key'':58B ''known'':73B ''labor'':66B ''landmarks'':111B ''landscap'':94B,106B ''largest'':31B ''lat'':219B,232B ''leaving'':208B ''legion'':124B ''locat'':36B ''location'':78B ''maneuvers'':147B ''manor'':116B ''meaning'':3B ''moonmoth'':122B ''mountain'':207B ''nerevarin'':171B ''odai'':50B ''of'':22B,41B,48B,95B,160B,187B,205B,225B ''on'':45B ''operat'':181B ''or'':5B,242B ''outland'':175B ''over'':213B ''persist'':216B ''play'':85B,165B ''political'':93B ''pow'':26B ''projects'':144B ''prominent'':14B ''prophecy'':172B,180B ''rebuilt'':212B ''red'':197B,206B ''redor'':132B ''region'':103B ''remains'':234B ''residential'':65B ''resilienc'':226B ''riv'':39B,51B ''rol'':88B,168B ''rugg'':105B ''ruins'':211B ''s'':25B,104B ''seat'':21B ''second'':30B ''second-largest'':29B ''serving'':18B ''settlement'':32B ''significant'':110B ''stat'':230B ''stoneforest'':6B ''stonewood'':4B ''strategic'':76B,142B ''stronghold'':133B ''style'':82B ''suggesting'':239B ''symbol'':224B ''templ'':119B ''the'':8B,20B,28B,38B,42B,46B,49B,52B,60B,64B,69B,90B,102B,113B,117B,121B,149B,170B,174B,179B,185B,191B,196B,203B,218B ''third'':150B ''though'':227B ''thre'':57B ''through'':141B ''tim'':214B ''to'':177B,202B ''tong'':11B ''town'':67B,72B ''trad'':77B,161B ''transform'':137B ''tribunal'':118B ''under'':154B,184B ''valley'':40B ''vital'':87B ''vivec'':34B ''vvardenfell'':17B,96B ''was'':12B,54B,128B,136B,193B ''west'':43B ''when'':173B ''with'':236B ''year'':198B ''years'':233B', 'scholar, darkelf', 'Balmora, located in Vvardenfell''s West Gash region, was the second-largest city after Vivec City and the seat of House Hlaalu''s power. Known for its strategic trade location and Hlaalu-style architecture, it played a vital role in Vvardenfell''s economy and politics. Historically, it was a House Redoran stronghold before House Hlaalu transformed it into a thriving center during the Third Era. Balmora was also significant in the Nerevarine Prophecy, serving as the base for the outlander destined to fulfill it. After being devastated during the Red Year in 4E 5, the city was rebuilt, continuing to stand as a symbol of resilience despite conflicting reports of its later status.', '', '', 'locationother');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('seyda_neen', 'Seyda Neen, often called the "Gateway to Vvardenfell," was a small yet significant port village in the southern Bitter Coast region, renowned for its lighthouse, the Grand Pharos. Originating from a House Hlaalu fleet''s ill-fated voyage to seek the "face of Veloth," the settlement was constructed from the remnants of the destroyed fleet. Over time, Seyda Neen became a vital stop for Imperial travelers heading to Vvardenfell, with its port managed by House Hlaalu and protected by the Imperial Legion. The village featured Imperial-style architecture built by the Gold Coast Trading Company, a Census and Excise Office for processing visitors, and a silt strider port providing connections to key destinations like Vivec and Balmora.

By the late Third Era, Seyda Neen served as a bustling hub for maritime activity and trade, though its surrounding swamplands earned it the nickname "Swamp Fever Capital of the World." Wildlife such as cliff racers, slaughterfish, and scribs populated the area, and Imperial cutters patrolled the waters to combat smuggling. However, Seyda Neen''s fate remains uncertain following the Red Year (4E 5) and the catastrophic eruption of Red Mountain, which devastated Vvardenfell and surrounding regions. Given its proximity to Vivec City, which bore the brunt of the disaster, it is unlikely Seyda Neen survived intact, leaving its legacy shrouded in mystery.', '''4e'':184B ''5'':185B ''activity'':136B ''and'':80B,102B,108B,120B,137B,159B,164B,186B,196B ''architectur'':92B ''are'':163B ''as'':130B,155B ''balmor'':121B ''becam'':63B ''bitt'':21B ''bor'':206B ''brunt'':208B ''built'':93B ''bustling'':132B ''by'':77B,82B,94B,122B ''call'':6B ''capital'':149B ''catastrophic'':188B ''census'':101B ''city'':204B ''cliff'':156B ''coast'':22B,97B ''combat'':171B ''company'':99B ''connections'':114B ''construct'':51B ''cutters'':166B ''destinations'':117B ''destroy'':57B ''devastat'':194B ''disast'':211B ''earn'':143B ''eruption'':189B ''excis'':103B ''fac'':45B ''fat'':40B,177B ''featur'':88B ''fev'':148B ''fleet'':36B,58B ''following'':180B ''for'':25B,67B,105B,134B ''from'':32B,52B ''gateway'':8B ''giv'':199B ''gold'':96B ''grand'':29B ''heading'':70B ''hlaalu'':35B,79B ''hous'':34B,78B ''howev'':173B ''hub'':133B ''ill'':39B ''ill-fat'':38B ''imperial'':68B,84B,90B,165B ''imperial-styl'':89B ''in'':18B,223B ''intact'':218B ''is'':213B ''it'':144B,212B ''its'':26B,74B,140B,200B,220B ''key'':116B ''lat'':124B ''leaving'':219B ''legacy'':221B ''legion'':85B ''lighthous'':27B ''lik'':118B ''manag'':76B ''maritim'':135B ''mountain'':192B ''mystery'':224B ''neen'':2A,4B,62B,128B,175B,216B ''nicknam'':146B ''of'':46B,55B,150B,190B,209B ''offic'':104B ''often'':5B ''originating'':31B ''over'':59B ''patroll'':167B ''phar'':30B ''populat'':161B ''port'':16B,75B,112B ''processing'':106B ''protect'':81B ''providing'':113B ''proximity'':201B ''racers'':157B ''red'':182B,191B ''region'':23B ''regions'':198B ''remains'':178B ''remnants'':54B ''renown'':24B ''s'':37B,176B ''scribs'':160B ''seek'':43B ''serv'':129B ''settlement'':49B ''seyd'':1A,3B,61B,127B,174B,215B ''shroud'':222B ''significant'':15B ''silt'':110B ''slaughterfish'':158B ''small'':13B ''smuggling'':172B ''southern'':20B ''stop'':66B ''strid'':111B ''style'':91B ''such'':154B ''surrounding'':141B,197B ''surviv'':217B ''swamp'':147B ''swamplands'':142B ''the'':7B,19B,28B,44B,48B,53B,56B,83B,86B,95B,123B,145B,151B,162B,168B,181B,187B,207B,210B ''third'':125B ''though'':139B ''tim'':60B ''to'':9B,42B,71B,115B,170B,202B ''trad'':138B ''trading'':98B ''travelers'':69B ''uncertain'':179B ''unlikely'':214B ''veloth'':47B ''villag'':17B,87B ''visitors'':107B ''vital'':65B ''vivec'':119B,203B ''voyag'':41B ''vvardenfell'':10B,72B,195B ''was'':11B,50B ''waters'':169B ''which'':193B,205B ''wildlif'':153B ''with'':73B ''world'':152B ''year'':183B ''yet'':14B', 'scholar, darkelf', 'Seyda Neen, known as the "Gateway to Vvardenfell," was a small port village in the southern Bitter Coast region, famous for its Grand Pharos lighthouse. Built from the remnants of a destroyed House Hlaalu fleet, the village became a key stop for Imperial travelers heading to Vvardenfell, with its port managed by House Hlaalu and protected by the Imperial Legion. Over time, it grew into a hub for maritime activity and trade, though it was plagued by swamplands and wildlife, earning the nickname "Swamp Fever Capital of the World." Its fate after the Red Year in 4E 5 remains uncertain, as the eruption of Red Mountain likely devastated the area.', '', '', 'locationother');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('ebonheart', 'Castle Ebonheart, also referred to simply as Ebonheart, was the hub of Imperial authority on Vvardenfell. Located near Vivec City, it served as the administrative center for the Vvardenfell district under the leadership of Duke Vedam Dren and his Grand Council. The castle housed the Imperial Chapels, which supported the Duke, his retainers, and the garrison, and acted as a central base for the Imperial Cult. Security was paramount, with the Duke protected by an elite Imperial Guard unit and the Hawkmoth Legion stationed within the castle walls. Castle Ebonheart was a hub for Imperial and House Hlaalu affairs, as the Duke also held the title of Hlaalu Grandmaster. Adjacent to the castle were the East Empire Company offices, bolstered by Imperial security, and diplomatic missions from Skyrim and Black Marsh, the latter advocating for its citizens under Morrowind''s unique legal exemptions regarding slavery.

The castle''s origins date back to 2E 582, when Vivec, inspired by the mainland city of Ebonheart, decreed the creation of a counterpart on Vvardenfell. However, it was not until 3E 414, following Vvardenfell''s opening for settlement, that Castle Ebonheart was constructed. It quickly became the Imperial capital of the island and a focal point for governance, trade, and diplomacy. Tragically, the proximity of Castle Ebonheart to Vivec City likely led to its destruction in 4E 5, during the Red Year, when the Ministry of Truth fell, triggering the eruption of Red Mountain and devastating the region.', '''2e'':154B ''3e'':178B ''414'':179B ''4e'':224B ''5'':225B ''582'':155B ''acted'':59B ''adjacent'':111B ''administrativ'':26B ''advocating'':135B ''affairs'':100B ''also'':4B,104B ''an'':76B ''and'':39B,55B,58B,81B,97B,125B,130B,200B,207B,242B ''as'':8B,24B,60B,101B ''authority'':15B ''back'':152B ''bas'':63B ''becam'':193B ''black'':131B ''bolster'':121B ''by'':75B,122B,159B ''capital'':196B ''castl'':2B,44B,88B,90B,114B,148B,187B,213B ''cent'':27B ''central'':62B ''chapels'':48B ''citizens'':138B ''city'':21B,162B,217B ''company'':119B ''construct'':190B ''council'':42B ''counterpart'':170B ''creation'':167B ''cult'':67B ''dat'':151B ''decr'':165B ''destruction'':222B ''devastating'':243B ''diplomacy'':208B ''diplomatic'':126B ''district'':31B ''dren'':38B ''duk'':36B,52B,73B,103B ''during'':226B ''east'':117B ''ebonheart'':1A,3B,9B,91B,164B,188B,214B ''elit'':77B ''empir'':118B ''eruption'':238B ''exemptions'':144B ''fell'':235B ''focal'':202B ''following'':180B ''for'':28B,64B,95B,136B,184B,204B ''from'':128B ''garrison'':57B ''governanc'':205B ''grand'':41B ''grandmast'':110B ''guard'':79B ''hawkmoth'':83B ''held'':105B ''his'':40B,53B ''hlaalu'':99B,109B ''hous'':45B,98B ''howev'':173B ''hub'':12B,94B ''imperial'':14B,47B,66B,78B,96B,123B,195B ''in'':223B ''inspir'':158B ''island'':199B ''it'':22B,174B,191B ''its'':137B,221B ''latt'':134B ''leadership'':34B ''led'':219B ''legal'':143B ''legion'':84B ''likely'':218B ''locat'':18B ''mainland'':161B ''marsh'':132B ''ministry'':232B ''missions'':127B ''morrowind'':140B ''mountain'':241B ''near'':19B ''not'':176B ''of'':13B,35B,108B,163B,168B,197B,212B,233B,239B ''offic'':120B ''on'':16B,171B ''opening'':183B ''origins'':150B ''paramount'':70B ''point'':203B ''protect'':74B ''proximity'':211B ''quickly'':192B ''red'':228B,240B ''referr'':5B ''regarding'':145B ''region'':245B ''retainers'':54B ''s'':141B,149B,182B ''security'':68B,124B ''serv'':23B ''settlement'':185B ''simply'':7B ''skyrim'':129B ''slavery'':146B ''station'':85B ''support'':50B ''that'':186B ''the'':11B,25B,29B,33B,43B,46B,51B,56B,65B,72B,82B,87B,102B,106B,113B,116B,133B,147B,160B,166B,194B,198B,210B,227B,231B,237B,244B ''titl'':107B ''to'':6B,112B,153B,215B,220B ''trad'':206B ''tragically'':209B ''triggering'':236B ''truth'':234B ''under'':32B,139B ''uniqu'':142B ''unit'':80B ''until'':177B ''vedam'':37B ''vivec'':20B,157B,216B ''vvardenfell'':17B,30B,172B,181B ''walls'':89B ''was'':10B,69B,92B,175B,189B ''wer'':115B ''when'':156B,230B ''which'':49B ''with'':71B ''within'':86B ''year'':229B', 'scholar, darkelf', 'Castle Ebonheart, located near Vivec City on Vvardenfell, was the administrative center for the Imperial authority in the region, serving as the hub for governance, trade, and diplomacy under Duke Vedam Dren. It housed the Imperial Chapels and the Duke''s elite guard, along with offices for the East Empire Company and diplomatic missions. Founded in 3E 414, the castle was the focal point of Imperial and House Hlaalu affairs. Tragically, it was destroyed in 4E 5 during the Red Year, when the eruption of Red Mountain ravaged the region.', '', '', 'locationother');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('blacklight', '
Blacklight, situated in northwestern Morrowind, serves as the capital of House Redoran and became the provincial capital after the sacking of Mournhold during the Accession War in 4E 6. The city is renowned for its disciplined architecture, hedge maze parks, and bustling harbors. Strategically located near the Velothi Mountains, it connects House Redoran’s expansive holdings across the region. The Rootspire, a central landmark, now hosts the Council of the Great Houses, solidifying Blacklight as the political and cultural heart of Morrowind.

Throughout history, Blacklight has symbolized Redoran resilience and honor. While raiders and bandits plagued the surrounding region in the First Era, the city endured and thrived, withstanding conflicts such as Tiber Septim''s invasion and the cataclysmic Red Year in 4E 5. By the Fourth Era, Blacklight''s size and grandeur rivaled that of the fallen Mournhold, reflecting centuries of growth and the Redoran ethos of perseverance and strength.', '''4e'':29B,124B ''5'':125B ''6'':30B ''accession'':26B ''across'':58B ''after'':19B ''and'':14B,42B,79B,91B,95B,108B,118B,133B,145B,151B ''architectur'':38B ''as'':8B,76B,113B ''bandits'':96B ''becam'':15B ''blacklight'':1A,2B,75B,86B,130B ''bustling'':43B ''by'':126B ''capital'':10B,18B ''cataclysmic'':120B ''central'':64B ''centuri'':142B ''city'':32B,106B ''conflicts'':111B ''connects'':52B ''council'':69B ''cultural'':80B ''disciplin'':37B ''during'':24B ''endur'':107B ''ethos'':148B ''expansiv'':56B ''fall'':139B ''first'':103B ''for'':35B ''fourth'':128B ''grandeur'':134B ''great'':72B ''growth'':144B ''harbors'':44B ''heart'':81B ''hedg'':39B ''history'':85B ''holdings'':57B ''honor'':92B ''hosts'':67B ''hous'':12B,53B,73B ''in'':4B,28B,101B,123B ''invasion'':117B ''is'':33B ''it'':51B ''its'':36B ''landmark'':65B ''locat'':46B ''maz'':40B ''morrowind'':6B,83B ''mountains'':50B ''mournhold'':23B,140B ''near'':47B ''northwestern'':5B ''now'':66B ''of'':11B,22B,70B,82B,137B,143B,149B ''parks'':41B ''perseveranc'':150B ''plagu'':97B ''political'':78B ''provincial'':17B ''raiders'':94B ''red'':121B ''redor'':13B,54B,89B,147B ''reflecting'':141B ''region'':60B,100B ''renown'':34B ''resilienc'':90B ''rival'':135B ''rootspir'':62B ''s'':55B,116B,131B ''sacking'':21B ''septim'':115B ''serv'':7B ''situat'':3B ''siz'':132B ''solidifying'':74B ''strategically'':45B ''strength'':152B ''such'':112B ''surrounding'':99B ''symboliz'':88B ''that'':136B ''the'':9B,16B,20B,25B,31B,48B,59B,61B,68B,71B,77B,98B,102B,105B,119B,127B,138B,146B ''thriv'':109B ''throughout'':84B ''tib'':114B ''velothi'':49B ''war'':27B ''whil'':93B ''withstanding'':110B ''year'':122B', 'scholar, darkelf', 'Blacklight, the capital of House Redoran in northwestern Morrowind, became the provincial capital after the fall of Mournhold in 4E 6. Known for its disciplined architecture, hedge maze parks, and bustling harbors, it serves as the political and cultural center of Morrowind. Key landmarks include the Rootspire, which now houses the Council of the Great Houses. Throughout history, Blacklight has symbolized Redoran resilience, surviving invasions, banditry, and the Red Year, growing into a city that rivals Mournhold''s former grandeur by the Fourth Era.', '', '', 'locationother');
INSERT INTO public.oghma VALUES ('clockwork_city', '
The Clockwork City, created by Sotha Sil, is a mechanical realm designed to replicate Nirn''s natural world in miniature while striving for perfection through the fusion of magic and technology. It exists outside conventional space and time, accessible through magical means like the Clockwork Globe. The city consists of three main regions: the Radius, a wilderness emulating Tamriel''s biomes; the Brass Fortress, the central settlement; and the Mechanical Fundament, housing Sotha Sil''s control center, the Throne Aligned. The City is maintained by structures like the Halls of Regulation, which sustain its ecosystem, and the Mnemonic Planisphere, which stores Sotha Sil’s memories. The Clockwork Apostles, its inhabitants, strive to create a "perfect Nirn" by blending mer and machine.

Throughout history, the Clockwork City has been a site of conflict and innovation. Sotha Sil modeled much of the City''s technology on Dwemer designs but ultimately evolved a unique style. Significant events include the creation of the Mechanical Heart, a replacement for the Heart of Lorkhan, and Sotha Sil''s murder by Almalexia in 3E 427. The City faced threats from Daedric Princes, rogue Apostles, and internal strife. Despite these challenges, it remains a marvel of invention, home to Fabricants, Factotums, and a society that reveres Sotha Sil''s vision. By the Fourth Era, the Mechanical Heart sustained the City, enabling its unique synthetic ecosystem to thrive even after Sotha Sil''s death.', '''3e'':179B ''427'':180B ''access'':41B ''after'':233B ''align'':82B ''almalexi'':177B ''and'':32B,39B,70B,98B,122B,135B,171B,190B,206B ''apostl'':110B,189B ''been'':130B ''biom'':63B ''blending'':120B ''brass'':65B ''but'':149B ''by'':7B,87B,119B,176B,215B ''cent'':79B ''central'':68B ''challeng'':195B ''city'':2A,5B,50B,84B,128B,143B,182B,224B ''clockwork'':1A,4B,47B,109B,127B ''conflict'':134B ''consists'':51B ''control'':78B ''conventional'':37B ''creat'':6B,115B ''creation'':159B ''daedric'':186B ''death'':237B ''design'':14B ''designs'':148B ''despit'':193B ''dwem'':147B ''ecosystem'':97B,229B ''emulating'':60B ''enabling'':225B ''even'':232B ''events'':156B ''evolv'':151B ''exists'':35B ''fabricants'':204B ''fac'':183B ''factotums'':205B ''for'':25B,166B ''fortress'':66B ''fourth'':217B ''from'':185B ''fundament'':73B ''fusion'':29B ''glob'':48B ''halls'':91B ''heart'':163B,168B,221B ''history'':125B ''hom'':202B ''housing'':74B ''in'':21B,178B ''includ'':157B ''inhabitants'':112B ''innovation'':136B ''internal'':191B ''invention'':201B ''is'':10B,85B ''it'':34B,196B ''its'':96B,111B,226B ''lik'':45B,89B ''lorkh'':170B ''machin'':123B ''magic'':31B ''magical'':43B ''main'':54B ''maintain'':86B ''marvel'':199B ''means'':44B ''mechanical'':12B,72B,162B,220B ''memori''